WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.220
I'm Nick Montfort, Professor of Digital Media
in Comparative Media Studies and Writing.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.070
I also teach at the School for Poetic Computation
in New York.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.890
I studied creative computing and developed
computation art in poetry.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:18.630
My computer generated poetry books include
#!, The Truelist, which is the first in the

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:23.470
recent "Using Electricity" series from Counterpath,
and Hard West Turn.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:30.010
My digital projects, including The Deletionist,
Sea and Spar Between, and the translation

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.160
project Renderings.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:37.859
These are all collaborations, are available
at NickM.com.

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:43.350
And I have MIT Press books, some collaborative,
some individually authored, which are The

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:50.550
New Media Reader, Twisty Little Passages,
Racing the Beam, 10 PRINT CHR$(205.5+RND(1)),

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:56.409
GOTO 10-- that's the reason why I have to
introduce myself, by the way-- Exploratory

00:00:56.409 --> 00:01:01.179
Programming for the Arts and Humanities, and
most recently, The Future.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:08.010
So I'll next introduce Stefan Helmreich, who's
on the opposite side of the table, who is

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.490
the Eltin E. Morrison Professor of Anthropology.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:14.060
He received his PhD in anthropology from Stanford.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.850
His research is focused on how biologists
think through the limits of life as a category

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.650
of analysis, which he's taken up in three
books-- Alien Ocean, Anthropological Voyages

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:31.210
in Microbial Seas; Silicon Second Nature,
Cultering Artificial Life in a Digital World;

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.030
and most recently, Sounding the Limits of
Life, Essays in the Anthropology of Biology

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.530
and Beyond.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:43.710
While on sabbatical this semester, Stefan
holds fellowships from the John Simon Guggenheim

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.900
Foundation and at the Radcliffe Institute
for Advanced Studies, where he's completing

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:53.990
a manuscript that offers an ethnographic account
of how scientists measure, model, and monitor

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.660
ocean waves in an era of climate change.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.030
Even though he is on sabbatical with these
two very, very desirable fellowships, he's

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.680
kind enough to join us for the panel.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.330
So an extra measure of thanks to Stefan for
being with us today.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.980
Next to me, Adam Haar Horowitz is a Masters
student in the Fluid Interfaces Group at the

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:13.980
Media Lab.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.930
His work aims to augment human awareness,
translating advances in neuroscience into

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.230
the design of interventions and interactive
experiences across the arts and sciences.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.950
He works on how technology can show us parts
of ourselves that remain otherwise invisible,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.319
allowing for introspection, wellness, and
wonder.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:35.819
He has background as a neuroscience tech at
MIT McGovern Institute, studying mindfulness

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:41.739
meditation and mind wandering, and as an artist
scientist at the Marina Abramovic Institute,

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:44.379
tying together neuroscience and performance
art.

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:48.620
His work has been shown at the Cannes Film
Festival, Transmediale, South by Southwest,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.329
the Beijing Media Biennale, SESC Pompeia,
and the Boston Museum of Fine Arts.

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:58.340
Here at MIT, he leads the Skunk Works team
of MIT Hacking Arts.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.670
His current projects include the microscopic
virtual reality adventures inside the human

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:09.349
body, along with dream control and capture
in the liminal space between wakefulness and

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:10.349
sleep.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:15.090
Caroline A. Jones, far end of the table, studies
modern and contemporary art with a particular

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.879
focus on its technological modes of production,
distribution, and reception.

00:03:19.879 --> 00:03:25.159
She is professor of art history in HTC, the
History, Theory, and Criticism of Architecture

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:26.340
and Art Program.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.450
She's trained in visual studies and art history
at Harvard, doing graduate work at the Institute

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.780
of Fine Arts in New York, before completing
her PhD at Stanford University.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.870
Previous to this, she worked in museum administration
and exhibit curation, holding positions at

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:46.329
the Museum of Modern Art in New York at the
Harvard University Art Museums, while she

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:48.849
also completed two documentary films.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:54.030
Beyond her work at those institutions, her
exhibitions and films have been shown at the

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.689
San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, the Hirshhorn
Museum and Sculpture Garden in Washington,

00:03:57.689 --> 00:04:03.480
DC, the Hara Museum Tokyo, Boston University
Art Gallery, and MIT's List Visual Art Center,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.599
among other venues.

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:09.950
Caroline has written and edited a number of
books, most recently The Global Work Of Art,

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:20.020
which focuses on her ongoing research interests,
including globalism, the agency of the artist,

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.020
and new media art.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:24.650
And finally, Agnieszka Kurant is an interdisciplinary
conceptual artist who examines how complex

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:29.830
social, economic, and cultural systems can
operate in ways that confuse distinctions

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.210
between fiction and reality, or nature and
culture.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:38.930
Her work investigates the economy of the invisible,
in which immaterial and imaginary entities,

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:44.560
fictions, famines, energy flows, and emergent
processes influence political, economic, and

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:45.930
ecological systems.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.350
She often collaborates with scientists, including
biologists, anthropologists, cartographers,

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.650
economists, computer engineers, and roboticists.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:05:00.210
Many of her works simulate nature and behave
like living organisms, self-organized complex

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.720
systems, or bachelor machines.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:10.040
In 2015, she did a commission for the facade
of the Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum in New

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.040
York.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.800
2013 to 2014, she presented a major solo exhibition
at the sculpture center there in New York.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.669
In 2010, she co-represented Poland at the
Venice Biennale of Architecture.

00:05:20.669 --> 00:05:26.949
Her most recent exhibitions include commissions
for the Guggenheim Bilbao, SF MoMA, Cleveland

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:36.490
Triennial, and La Panacee, Montpelier, as
well solo shows at SCAD MoA, and CCA in Tel

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:37.490
Aviv.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.000
Her work's also been exhibited at Palais de
Tokyo, the Tate Modern, the Kitchen, Witte

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.789
de With, Frieze Projects Projects, Moderna
Museet, MUMOK, Grazer Kunstverein, and Bonner

00:05:45.789 --> 00:05:52.389
Kunstverein, Stroom den Haag, and Performa
Biennial.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:06:01.020
Agnieszka is currently the Ida Ely Rubin Artist
in Residence at MIT'S CAST, and holds scholarships

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:06.889
at the Smithsonian Institute and the [INAUDIBLE]
Institute.

00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:12.699
Well, as you can guess, we have a group with
some relevant expertise and perspective, but

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:15.030
also a diverse group.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:21.370
And I'd say that the four of you, certainly
from different perspectives, you undertake

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:26.010
the types of inquiry, exploring, asking questions,
generating new sorts of insights.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:31.830
This is something I think unites people at
the university generally, even if they're

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.370
doing very different sorts of activities.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.060
They're somehow undertaking inquiry.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:42.439
So since our occasion here is discussing Agnieszka's
work at MIT as an artist, I'd like to ask

00:06:42.439 --> 00:06:48.650
how she inquires through art, and then invite
the others of you to explain how artistic

00:06:48.650 --> 00:07:02.699
inquiry is similar to or different from the
types of inquiry that you pursue.

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:22.770
Thank you so much for hosting this wonderful
panel.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:28.639
Thank you, everyone here, for joining me.

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:37.060
The point of departure of my work is the phenomenon
that nature and culture that are examples

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.039
of emergence and collective intelligence.

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:47.810
These phenomena happen in various complex
systems, the complex systems that can be a

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:55.009
termite colony, a human society, human brain,
the internet, the urban fabric, or even the

00:07:55.009 --> 00:07:56.009
stock exchange.

00:07:56.009 --> 00:08:01.949
There are systems with many components and
factors that are so complicated that are often

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:04.689
very difficult to anticipate what's going
to happen in them.

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:09.319
They evolve in a nonlinear way.

00:08:09.319 --> 00:08:12.719
And I've been investigating these systems.

00:08:12.719 --> 00:08:17.434
I want to show very quickly a couple of slides
of my points of departure of a couple of my

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:18.479
works.

00:08:18.479 --> 00:08:22.759
I'm going to just comment with like one sentence
per image.

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:31.181
So this is an organism that inspired a lot
of my works, slime mold, that doesn't have

00:08:31.181 --> 00:08:37.269
any nervous system, but consists of singular
cells that, when confronted with some form

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:44.139
of danger-- lack of nutrients or water-- aggregate
into a super organism, into one unit, and

00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:49.440
moves as one unit, and displays signs of intelligence.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.279
So it can even go through a maze.

00:08:51.279 --> 00:08:56.730
And in fact, it was co-opted for computation
by scientists such as [? Andrea ?] [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:03.839
working in Great Britain, who was doing certain--
who was writing the Center for Unconventional

00:09:03.839 --> 00:09:11.440
Computing using slime mold and other organisms
to perform various operations.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.800
Another example of emergency and collective
intelligence in cultural bacteria are colonies

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.680
that perform groupthink.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:26.410
And the work of Eshel Ben-Jacob, an Israeli
scientist who discovered that certain bacteria,

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:32.930
for example, make collective decisions where
they, let's say, decide to kill off half of

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:35.660
the colony for the collective good, future
good of the remaining bacteria.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:46.970
And so it's kind of like, in fact, a visualization
of a groupthink, one can consider.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:54.360
This is a social phenomenon that happened
on the platform called Reddit about a year

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.360
ago.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.420
About 1 million people participated in it.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:03.170
It was a sort of virtual fictional country,
universe, called The Place, where each participant

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:09.910
could place a single pixel wherever they wanted
in this virtual space.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.149
And various emergent self-organized processes
emerged.

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:18.089
So a lot of a fictional characters, fictional
nations, cults.

00:10:18.089 --> 00:10:21.839
This is an example of one of them.

00:10:21.839 --> 00:10:25.360
This is the Black Void.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:32.610
Another point of departure for a lot of my
research is this branch of science that analyzes

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.500
and grows artificial societies.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:39.160
And it started in the early '90s, firstly
at the Santa Fe Institute by two scientists,

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:40.410
Epstein and Axtell.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:48.950
But some of this earlier research has been
done by the economist Thomas Schelling.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:57.300
It's basically a way of using algorithms to
calculate very complex systems of how societies

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:03.440
will behave, and the interplay between individual
decisions of citizens in a society.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:10.260
So here are some examples of one of my works
using the artificial society models based

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.910
on the Thomas Schelling, the economist, his
model of self-segregation and how it can actually

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:21.470
be computed.

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:32.110
These are the works that stem from all this
research.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:39.770
Sorry, I think we lost the image.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:40.770
OK, thank you.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:47.050
So these are the works-- this is a series
called [? AAI, ?] which are works that I outsourced--

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.260
crowdsourced-- to non-human agents.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:55.800
Because I'm interested in also forms of non-human
intelligence.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:02.810
In the times when other scientists are talking
about the fact that the intelligence is not

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:08.889
what the mostly Western culture assumes to
be individual and located in the brain, but

00:12:08.889 --> 00:12:10.040
on the contrary.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:15.920
It's distributed, and very external to the
brain, and it doesn't have to be human.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.079
So a lot of my work are also taking this as
point of departure.

00:12:20.079 --> 00:12:26.389
And so these works were outsourced to the
colonies of living termites.

00:12:26.389 --> 00:12:31.769
Each sculpture is built by a colony of approximately
a million termites that were supplied with

00:12:31.769 --> 00:12:40.000
alternative materials, such as pigmented sands,
particles of gold and crystals. and each colony

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.820
is isolated for about three, four months.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.110
And they just built.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:47.630
And each form is different.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.389
Nothing can be anticipated.

00:12:50.389 --> 00:12:54.350
And there in this hybrid forms that hover
somewhere between nature and culture.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:13:00.399
And what is really interesting that the entomologists
point out how each single colony of exactly

00:13:00.399 --> 00:13:05.529
the same species produces very different shapes
of termite mounds, which also corresponds

00:13:05.529 --> 00:13:09.370
to a different collective personality of each
colony.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:13.079
So just like humans, they can represent different
collective personalities.

00:13:13.079 --> 00:13:19.880
Or just like social movements that sometimes
can be dynamic and fast, sometimes more slow,

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:23.240
dispersed, or concentrated, headless or not.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:30.529
So these works also related to my interest
in self-exploitations present in society,

00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:40.230
especially by corporations using algorithms
to harvest data of citizens who are completely

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:41.230
unaware of this.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:44.740
So this is like a private distributed form
of self-exploitation.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:52.790
That's a continuation of this work where I
am pouring hot zinc into abandoned termite

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:53.790
mounds.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:59.130
And they form this negative casts of various--
so they're like internal portraits of various

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.130
societies.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:04.810
They are poured in an African desert.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:11.959
And these works, entitled The End of Signature,
that I've done various iterations of, are

00:14:11.959 --> 00:14:22.170
related to my reflections about how the aggregated
value of social capital can not only be exploited,

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:26.970
which is unfortunately happening so often
in contemporary societies, but it can also

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:33.540
be precisely calculated and can be used or
analyzed for a good cause, and that we should

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.019
maybe try to embrace it.

00:14:36.019 --> 00:14:42.660
So what I'm doing in this work is I developed,
together with computer programmers, an algorithm

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:48.820
that morphs thousands of signatures that we
first collect and scan into a collective signature.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:53.540
And I've developed these collective signatures
for various social movements, communities,

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.500
and social groups.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:00.240
The first one was a facade of the Guggenheim
Museum in New York.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:07.139
And this is a collective portrait of all the
visitors, kind of alluding to the fact that

00:15:07.139 --> 00:15:13.190
viewership today also became a form of labor,
visible labor, because audiences are constantly

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:15.589
Instagramming images and circulating images.

00:15:15.589 --> 00:15:21.029
And it became a sort of form of invisible
labor and contributing to the value of the

00:15:21.029 --> 00:15:22.029
artwork.

00:15:22.029 --> 00:15:27.529
This is on the facade of the Cleveland Museum
of Art, where this is all the employees of

00:15:27.529 --> 00:15:29.450
this museum who donated their signatures.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:36.760
And this is a community of people living in
one building in Utrecht, which is like a last,

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:44.449
final island of public housing in the middle
of a privatized public space in central Utrecht.

00:15:44.449 --> 00:15:49.990
And this a project that also using aggregation
of human behavior.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:56.740
But in this case, it produced a certain level
of a collective Tamagotchi, or an organism,

00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:03.000
or animatronic soccer ball in this case, that
is entitled a Quasi Object, making the reference

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:08.720
to Michel Serres' idea of how certain objects
need to be activated as they're circulated

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:09.720
between agents.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:15.470
So different movements of soccer balls from
games played online by various internet users

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:21.810
who are aggregated and-- I don't believe that
we can play it, but it's moving-- actually,

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:25.600
it's an animatronic piece.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:34.759
And finally, the series of works that I realized
here at MIT, they started with a series called

00:16:34.759 --> 00:16:39.959
Production Line, where I started outsourcing
my works through human agents, multiplicity

00:16:39.959 --> 00:16:46.500
of human agents, working on this new platform
that, in my opinion, is the beginning of the

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:50.810
new working class.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:57.160
The platform is called Amazon Mechanical Turk,
but there are various similar forms that exist.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.370
And they allow for crowdsourced labor, people
working on small tasks in front of their computer

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:03.850
screens online around the world.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:08.510
And this platform can often, although not
always, become exploitative.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:13.789
But could also be, possibly in the future,
used for the public good, because it allows

00:17:13.789 --> 00:17:14.789
for collective intelligence experiments.

00:17:14.789 --> 00:17:16.120
And it has been used for some social experiments.

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:24.240
So I took advantage of this platform to crowdsource
some of the works.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.530
Thousands of people also working worldwide.

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:34.360
And the series is called Production Line,
where each worker was asked to draw a single

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:35.360
line.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:42.110
And then they were algorithmically fused into
these collective drawings that were then outputted,

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:47.390
as you can see, with a pen plotter as one
single line, continuous line.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:56.049
So each drawing represents an amalgamated
labor of, in this case, 500, 1,000, or 2,000

00:17:56.049 --> 00:18:01.690
people, that each line has been drawn in a
different part of the world.

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:08.700
And what's most important about these works
is that I created a system that allows for

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:10.620
profit sharing.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:15.750
So in a way, it allows for siphoning off the
money from the art market and redistributing

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:17.240
it among these workers.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:19.450
So they are participating in the profits.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:24.260
And when each of these works sells-- they
are being paid on the spot as they complete

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:25.260
their work.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:31.559
But additionally, if the work sells, the proceeds
are evenly distributed among all the participants,

00:18:31.559 --> 00:18:36.510
because Amazon Mechanical Turk platform allows
for storing their IP addresses.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:43.190
So we can pay them bonuses at any point in
the future if the work sells.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.549
This is a project that I developed here already
at MIT.

00:18:47.549 --> 00:18:55.190
It was a generous collaboration from the CSAIL
team, Professor Boris Katz who is present

00:18:55.190 --> 00:19:00.530
here, and also David Mayo and Andrei Barbu.

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:10.150
And this work is entitled Assembly Line, and
continues the idea of how to subvert the system

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:11.790
of like self-exploitation.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:19.460
So in this case, we worked on the ways in
which-- how people donate or volunteer their

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:21.760
data, their bio data, online.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:30.309
We transformed it into the ways in which people
taking selfies, or any kind of self-portraits,

00:19:30.309 --> 00:19:34.679
how they could be aggregated into a collective
self-portrait of this collective worker that

00:19:34.679 --> 00:19:36.559
this new working class constitutes.

00:19:36.559 --> 00:19:44.480
So what you're looking at is like an aggregation
of about 10,000 people-- photographs, self-portraits

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:47.590
of like 10,000 people that are aggregated
into one.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:58.440
And then, on the basis of several images that
we generated at CSAIL, I created these 3D

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:06.430
forms that are abstractions that represent
this collective self-portrait of this society.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:12.160
And this project is now evolving further,
where workers from the same platform are going

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:16.779
to put together abstract machines from different
machine parts.

00:20:16.779 --> 00:20:22.480
And then they will also be outputted in various
forms.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:30.140
And lastly, I would like to show just one
minute of the project that I did here at MIT

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:32.600
entitled Animal Internet.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:36.720
And I collaborated with Adam Haar Horowitz,
who's present here, as well as Agnes Cameron,

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:45.000
who I think is also here somewhere, and Owen
Trueblood and Ishaan Grover.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:52.610
And it's, in many ways, like a culmination
of all this research between non-human intelligence

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:55.370
and collective intelligence and artificial
intelligence.

00:20:55.370 --> 00:21:00.010
So what happens here is that, inspired by
the phenomena of animal internet, where a

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:08.720
lot of scientists are tracking animals all
over the world, on the one hand by putting

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:14.070
chips in animals and that they can be followed
digitally, but also by placing cameras in

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.179
jungles or in the North Pole.

00:21:15.179 --> 00:21:18.179
And watching completely wild animals without
their knowledge.

00:21:18.179 --> 00:21:24.910
Inspired by this phenomena, I realized that
since these animals have a kind of hybrid

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:29.940
life, because many of them are followed on
these webcams, they have websites, Facebook

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:35.610
pages, and a following of sometimes a couple
of hundred thousand people.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:41.810
So the creates this kind of hybrid reality
between wilderness and culture.

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:51.169
So what we did together with the Hacking Arts
team is we created this project which consists

00:21:51.169 --> 00:21:56.050
of two real webcams that are broadcasting
real wild animals.

00:21:56.050 --> 00:22:01.779
One is a tiger in the jungle and one is a
polar bear in the North Pole.

00:22:01.779 --> 00:22:07.430
And we, here at MIT, built two artificial
animals.

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:08.430
So they are animatronic animals.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:13.179
We used artificial fur, so no animals were
harmed.

00:22:13.179 --> 00:22:24.299
And basically, one of them, the one the upper
right corner, was powered by workers of the

00:22:24.299 --> 00:22:29.799
Amazon Mechanical Turk that sent, as their
task for they were paid, they sent information

00:22:29.799 --> 00:22:31.330
about how they were feeling.

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:35.900
Whether they were tired, hungry, angry, or
relaxed and happy.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:48.710
And these aggregated emotions, like through
sentiment analysis and different kind of programs

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:56.910
that we use, we converted into various behaviors
of this fictional animal in a kind jungle

00:22:56.910 --> 00:22:57.910
environment.

00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:04.279
And the other camera is swarm of gerbils,
which was in a desert environment.

00:23:04.279 --> 00:23:09.490
And this one is powered by a Twitter scraper
that we wrote.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:15.610
So basically, we were monitoring Twitter accounts
of all the protest movements in the world,

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:22.360
and what kind of sentiments they are expressing,
whether it's frustration, hope, aggression.

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:24.370
And whether it's positive or negative.

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:32.060
And so this aggregated data from different
social movements were then powering a sugarscape

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:40.750
model, an artificial society model, that was
translated to a collective behavior of this

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:41.750
swarm.

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:43.500
Thank you.

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:44.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:47.160
Thanks very much.

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:51.679
So first of all, I wanted to know, there's
five chairs I can see.

00:23:51.679 --> 00:23:56.360
Please come-- and you don't even have to sit
in the front row.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.440
Please come and occupy them.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:02.990
And thanks very much, Agnieszka for-- so you
showed some images of your work, you told

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:04.549
us about your process and your interests.

00:24:04.549 --> 00:24:07.210
But I wanted to ask one more thing.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:14.200
I wanted to get you to say something about
how you work as an artist to do inquiries

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:17.850
specifically, because I've asked the others
to respond to this topic, like how the way

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:23.230
in which they inquire is different or similar
to your artistic inquiry.

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:24.230
So can you tell us?

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:25.230
Sure.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:30.940
I mean, I was hoping that, kind of, this was
my answer, but of course-- well, I mean, I

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:32.640
collaborate with various scientists.

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.960
But I'm trying for my work not to be illustrative.

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:37.559
So it's always somehow twisted.

00:24:37.559 --> 00:24:43.790
And I really like crossovers between different
things that maybe wouldn't crossover in real

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:44.790
life.

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:47.580
And to establish connections or dialogues
between sometimes scientists that maybe wouldn't

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:52.679
normally meet and wouldn't necessarily be
in a dialogue.

00:24:52.679 --> 00:25:00.210
And even if it leads to miscommunications
or errors, but these errors could be productive.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:02.900
So it's never-- although it is a collaboration
with scientists and other professionals, there

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:13.080
is always a very strong component of errorism,
as I call it, and irrationality, and slippages.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:16.320
And it's just unexpected, non-linear things
that can happen.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:19.220
Well, thanks very much.

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:23.929
I think that that's very consistent with what
you've shown, but you've highlighted these

00:25:23.929 --> 00:25:26.669
two aspects that your work is collaborative.

00:25:26.669 --> 00:25:31.610
It's not just the termites who are working
together with the slime mold, but that you're

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:37.340
working collaboratively and you're also interested
in the unexpected.

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:47.060
So let me ask for, perhaps Stefan, would you
like to ... next.

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:48.060
Sure.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:49.060
Thanks.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:53.260
And thank you, Agnes, again for that display
of incredible works.

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:55.710
Inquiry-- that's your question?

00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:57.299
Yes.

00:25:57.299 --> 00:26:03.429
I'm an anthropologist, and so my mode of inquiry
is ethnographic fieldwork.

00:26:03.429 --> 00:26:08.630
And I spend time with people, with collectives,
and try to figure out what they're doing by

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:12.049
doing participant observation, by interviewing
them.

00:26:12.049 --> 00:26:16.360
I spent a lot of time with scientists in particular.

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:22.670
And I bring a set of tools to that inquiry
which are things that I've gathered from the

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:29.320
social theory tool kit, which include analytics
of gender, and race, sexuality, ideas about

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:35.200
belief, ideas about conviction, hegemony,
practice, ideology, this whole kind of tool

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:36.600
kit of things.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:41.960
And the idea is to bring those with me into
the zone of inquiry, hanging out with people,

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.590
and see what they do.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:48.390
And sometimes they can explain things and
sometimes they break and they can't explain

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:51.330
things, or people give me other categories
and say, well, no, no, no.

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:56.230
This is not really about whatever you say
it's about.

00:26:56.230 --> 00:26:57.500
Really, it's about complexity.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:58.750
Or really, it's about nonlinearity.

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:03.120
Since I spend a lot of time studying scientists,
I got a lot of that.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.830
They say, no, no, no, you're saying that this
has to do with culture and power, but really,

00:27:06.830 --> 00:27:12.110
it's a scaling problem that's about a certain
set of non-linear dynamics, that properly

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:16.010
understood as emergent phenomena, would reveal
x, y, and z.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:21.120
And then I can speak back to that and say,
well, yes, no.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:27.991
So I think part of what I have in common with
Agnieszka, then, is that encounter with the

00:27:27.991 --> 00:27:28.991
unexpected.

00:27:28.991 --> 00:27:35.500
Just like this empirical work, and then the
attempt to find something and encounter something

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:38.510
that is surprising within that.

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:44.000
But then also just sometimes be assertive
about my own categories and say, this is patriarchy.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:49.480
What is going on with this Kavanaugh thing
at the moment, that is patriarchy.

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.900
I am not buying it, that he's crying.

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:52.900
Sorry.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:53.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:54.900
Anyway.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:55.900
It's his daughter's prayer.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:56.900
I know.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.570
His daughter's prayer.

00:27:58.570 --> 00:27:59.680
Yeah, so.

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:04.090
OK, I think-- so Caroline was originally signed
up to be our respondent, so I think I'll give

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:05.090
her the privilege of going last.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:10.620
And I'll give Adam the privilege of going
next.

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:12.720
Cool, thank you.

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:18.610
Yeah, I think that, if it's not immediately
apparent, I am at an earlier stage of question

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:24.360
asking than fellow panelists, so I will try
to contribute productive confusion, because

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:27.950
I am productively confused perhaps.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:34.690
My background at the moment is a mix of neuroscience
and art.

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:42.000
And I wanted to tough quickly on why I think
that's a worthwhile mix, and why it's a contribution

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:43.660
to this conversation.

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:48.620
I think neuroscience stands out, especially
stands out at an institute like this because

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:54.110
it is such an unstable meeting ground of objectivity
and subjectivity.

00:28:54.110 --> 00:28:58.779
It is an attempt at a third person study of
first person experience with first person

00:28:58.779 --> 00:28:59.779
experience.

00:28:59.779 --> 00:29:05.450
And it is so contingent and murky, it makes
it really exciting to study.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:13.510
It also means that you don't get to stand
on two stable feet in either the realm of

00:29:13.510 --> 00:29:15.909
the study of experience or the creation of
experience.

00:29:15.909 --> 00:29:23.750
So my interest and excitement about Agnieszka
work and modes of inquiry were similar in

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:26.720
that there's a mix of stability and instability.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:32.490
There's a clear picture that comes out of
a very murky collection of parts.

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:38.130
I'd say that it's something that's really
nice in terms of the collective piece that

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:46.049
this sort of subjective objective mix has
affected upon me is a sense of myself as collective.

00:29:46.049 --> 00:29:55.169
I studied here a few years ago in the neuroscience
department, largely neural networks, which

00:29:55.169 --> 00:30:01.340
changed my notion of self as a notion of there's
a me in a little bit of jelly.

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:06.750
And I've got to get it, and I'll get the jelly
eventually, to something more like I am temporal

00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:07.750
dynamics.

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:08.750
I am rhythm.

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:09.750
I am time.

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:10.750
I am waves.

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:17.100
So the movement from functional neural anatomy
as a sort of phrenology to one of, instead,

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:20.690
dynamic networks, myself as collective.

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:28.860
And then I take it even further to-- if you
see something like a commisurotomy patient

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:34.159
in the neurosciences, somebody's who's had
their hemispheres split, something that has

00:30:34.159 --> 00:30:37.830
stuck with me as a personal collective is
you might think that when you cut someone's

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:39.789
brain in half, it sort of turns off.

00:30:39.789 --> 00:30:40.789
It doesn't.

00:30:40.789 --> 00:30:46.470
Can keep functioning quite well, but some
develop alien hand syndrome, which people

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.049
might have heard of.

00:30:48.049 --> 00:30:51.590
But you develop, essentially, hands that disagrees.

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:54.530
So one might want to button up, one might
want to button down.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.700
You develop hands that have different information
schemes of the world.

00:30:57.700 --> 00:31:01.549
One is more verbal, one has more sensitive
motor information.

00:31:01.549 --> 00:31:04.211
And it's interesting that you can break in
that way.

00:31:04.211 --> 00:31:09.140
But it's also interesting that if you were
currently broken in that way, broken in terms

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:12.269
of being a collective, you wouldn't know.

00:31:12.269 --> 00:31:17.710
The notion that introspection as intentional
spotlight is a unifier of many selves.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:20.900
That I might have, at the moment-- oh, I shouldn't
poke that.

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:25.600
I might have, at the moment, a more verbal,
a younger and older Portuguese speaking and

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:29.840
English speaking, a mix of cells with a mix
of knowledges.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.769
And that my intentional spotlight is one of
those.

00:31:32.769 --> 00:31:38.559
And so what I see inside is a necessary unification
of a possible split collective, if that makes

00:31:38.559 --> 00:31:40.710
sense, has stuck with me in a big way.

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:42.330
And that's exciting.

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:49.029
I, at the moment, mostly study sleep, which
is also a weird way in which people break

00:31:49.029 --> 00:31:51.750
very predictably.

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:56.279
If you get people at the right state of sleep,
they will lose syntax.

00:31:56.279 --> 00:31:59.730
They will pull memories from different parts
of their lives that they wouldn't pull when

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:01.560
they were awake.

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:03.159
They'll be more fluid thinking.

00:32:03.159 --> 00:32:04.850
They'll be more rigid thinking.

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:07.720
I work specifically in a weird sleep state
called hypnagogia.

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:13.860
And in that sleep state, you have a really
deep inversion of an organizing principle

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:20.409
to a collective intelligence, which is that
when you're awake, as a machine, you're generally,

00:32:20.409 --> 00:32:25.299
I think, designed to associate two things
that are close together and make predictions

00:32:25.299 --> 00:32:27.240
about the world accordingly.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:31.770
There is an association in my head between
water bottle, water drinking, thirsty, et

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:32.770
cetera.

00:32:32.770 --> 00:32:35.779
I'll move towards it when one concept comes
up.

00:32:35.779 --> 00:32:37.970
In hypnagogia, you get a full inversion.

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:42.880
It becomes much harder for people to react
to things that are closely associated versus

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:43.970
associated at long distance.

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:49.760
So you have people have faster reaction times
when you associate a porpoise and a helicopter

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:52.399
than a porpoise and the ocean.

00:32:52.399 --> 00:32:55.860
And this is exciting to me, because it's like,
whoa, I'm a collective which I can't introspect

00:32:55.860 --> 00:33:00.429
on it, but I can get down to base rules and
switch those base rules and see a different

00:33:00.429 --> 00:33:02.549
collective in different states.

00:33:02.549 --> 00:33:05.649
If that makes sense, the sleeping self versus
not sleeping self.

00:33:05.649 --> 00:33:10.029
So the collective question touches on a lot
of things I'm excited about.

00:33:10.029 --> 00:33:14.210
Personal collectives, extended inactive intelligence,
which we'll probably get into later, because

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:15.789
I won't get into now.

00:33:15.789 --> 00:33:23.590
And then lastly, excites me because the reason
that I'm here is that Agnieszka and Agnes

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:30.690
and Ishaan and many of us, and Gary and Leila
got to collaborate on a project in hacking

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:31.690
arts.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:35.760
And I think that hacking arts is a sort of
nice demonstration of a certain organizing

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:40.460
principle of emergence, where I'm not a personal
believer in the idea that you can take a bunch

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:45.060
of dumb parts and put them together and get
something automatically intelligent without

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:46.060
some sort of organizing principle.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:48.630
But I think that hacking arts kind of turns
out like that.

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:52.460
You just throw a bunch of people who are really
different into a room and they kind of bounce

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:53.460
off each other.

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:55.240
And things pretty predictably come out.

00:33:55.240 --> 00:34:03.100
And that's really been very exciting for me
as a person who gets to guide that and can't

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:04.100
guide that.

00:34:04.100 --> 00:34:12.610
So yeah, I would say that hacking arts, for
me as a collective, there's the-- I think

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.920
it's Kahneman line about emergence.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:18.320
Oh no, it's Danny Hillis.

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:22.100
It's like how can a bunch of stupid things
come together and be smart?

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:25.420
I'm really interested in hacking arts, with
how can a bunch of smart things come together

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:29.770
and be stupid, in terms of producing absurdity
or getting past utility.

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:31.050
If that makes sense.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:32.950
Hopefully that's what we're doing tonight.

00:34:32.950 --> 00:34:36.840
All of that to say I'm confused by my disciplinary
path.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:41.910
I think it's a productive confusion that contributes
to the notion of being a confused collective,

00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:46.410
which is how I introspect upon myself, and
also how I try to work in the world, which

00:34:46.410 --> 00:34:53.410
is what I think hacking arts does and I think
the project did.

00:34:53.410 --> 00:35:00.420
I think that's why I'm here.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:01.590
All right, thanks very much.

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:02.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:05.110
So Caroline can tell us some about her methods
of inquiry and her connection.

00:35:05.110 --> 00:35:08.010
Well, I'm actually very moved to respond to
Adam.

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:14.010
This is not the gestalt theory that I was
taught by Rudolf Arnheim.

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:19.150
So I'm really excited that neuroscience is
in a place where it can think about dynamic

00:35:19.150 --> 00:35:23.890
flow and collectivities that split and come
back together.

00:35:23.890 --> 00:35:27.970
I had Lydia bring in this video, which you
can watch.

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:31.140
And if you get bored with me, or even if you
don't.

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:39.080
Eventually about 15 seconds in, 20 seconds
in, you'll see the slime mold social amoeba

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:41.110
form an aggregate.

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:47.040
And what's amazing about this unicellular
organism that emerges into a multicellular

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:54.890
organism is that it does so while each cell
maintains its identity, as it were.

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:59.780
It doesn't presumably have consciousness as
we hubristically imagine it.

00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:07.880
But it's organizing itself into to this slug,
which is then going to move off to the left

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:15.260
as an organized entity looking for food, as
a collective that chemically directs itself

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:19.340
towards the food that it might be able to
sense in its medium.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:25.880
And what's cool about this scientific video
is that at the very end of this loop, you'll

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:30.930
see this slug become a vertical fruiting body.

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:36.840
So it actually is coming up vertically from
the agar plate right here and standing up

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:38.480
and going like, OK, I give up.

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.480
There's no food.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:41.400
I'm going to make spores.

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:43.150
I'm going to make spores.

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:49.060
And what's hilarious about the science of
this is that it's attracting people that are

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:58.490
just incapable of thinking of this without
words like altruism, and selfishness, and

00:36:58.490 --> 00:36:59.490
sacrifice.

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:05.240
So if you don't get to be spore, you lose,
Buckwheat, in the science about this kind

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:06.240
of material.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:12.080
So I look to culture, as an art historian,
I look historically to see what artists have

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:14.400
done with certain generative concepts.

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:18.940
And in that sense, I'm very much fond of Stefan's
tool kit.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:22.650
And we've collaborated on some things together
with great pleasure.

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:26.890
So I look historically at what they've left
us traces.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:30.490
Texts, images, journals, interviews.

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:37.140
And then as a critic in the contemporary world,
I work with contemporary artists whose work

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:42.650
excites me very much and seems to propel ethnography
of the future, if you will.

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:44.460
I mean, something coming into being.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:51.240
A kind of emergent thinking that I want to
be part of, or I'm want to see in the world.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:54.580
So Agnieszka's presentation was wonderful.

00:37:54.580 --> 00:38:02.180
Her article in Cabinet is very interesting
about the-- I have to read the title-- the

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:05.720
uncomputables.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:11.430
But where slime mold in her thinking is a
kind of model for a certain calculational

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:15.820
possibility, I'm actually looking for something
else in slime mold.

00:38:15.820 --> 00:38:21.380
So the artists working with biological material
that have taught me things, that have helped

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:27.980
me think, are ones that are crafting what
I've been calling biofiction.

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:34.470
And they've been doing so for-- OK, here's
the Utopian project-- a cultural evolution

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:38.510
that I want to see happen that I'm calling
symbiotics.

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:43.500
I just use whatever platform I have to tout
this stupid neologism.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:45.560
The idea of the ontic is that which is.

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:50.560
And the idea of symbiosis is that's what we
are.

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:53.500
We are slime mold.

00:38:53.500 --> 00:39:00.000
We are completely, pathetically dependent
on our environment, on our planetary systems,

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.580
on the scrum of life of which we are a part.

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:09.200
And the sooner we can get rid of the fantasy
of the selfish individual, the selfish gene,

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:12.900
whatever the selfish thing is, the sooner
we can get rid of that the better.

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:20.510
The sooner we can think together to sense,
somehow, our complete dependence on the planetary

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:26.140
systems that make our consciousness and our
lives possible, the better.

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:32.870
So art that does that is art that, at the
moment, I'm excited by and want to celebrate,

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:37.820
because I have another fantasy, which I'm
sure my anthropologist friend could disabuse

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:43.480
of, that we make art to externalize something
that then changes use.

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:47.230
That then evolves us to a different kind of
creature.

00:39:47.230 --> 00:39:49.020
So this is my fantasy.

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:54.400
And I celebrate that contemporary art as a
place that I don't know how to get to, but

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:55.730
they seem to be getting there.

00:39:55.730 --> 00:40:02.690
They seem to be emerging into that project
of collective-- I don't want to call it intelligence--

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:09.120
collective action, collective sensing, collective
being in a chemical bath, which is undertheorized.

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:12.770
The binary bits of neural nets.

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:18.970
Forget about the synapse, which is a gap that
has to be bridged by wet chemicals.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:25.120
So for me, these cultural conversations, these
metaphors we live by are metaphors that will

00:40:25.120 --> 00:40:29.290
help us possibly live in the future.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:33.020
And if we can't do it, we deserve to be extinct.

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:34.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:34.020 --> 00:40:35.020
Well, that's heartening.

00:40:35.020 --> 00:40:36.020
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:36.020 --> 00:40:44.710
I think that that needed to be heard.

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:48.440
And I would say that if I were to be an ethnographer,
I would want to be an ethnographer for the

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:49.440
future, definitely.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:51.920
So I appreciate that concept also.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:57.710
Actually, I want to ask, among the many topics
we have related to neuroscience and biology

00:40:57.710 --> 00:41:05.990
and your artistic connection to science, I
wanted to talk about one related to culture

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:08.770
and power.

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:14.850
Specifically that a lot of the formulations
of collectively that we look to, things like

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:19.920
Amazon Mechanical Turk, are pretty hierarchical.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:28.290
Even something like Wikipedia, while it isn't
modeled in the same way with the payment structure,

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:30.880
there are definitely heirarchies and heterarchies
at the very least of power, authority, expertise.

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:41.050
And those are reflected in the content of
that, in many ways, miraculous project, but

00:41:41.050 --> 00:41:45.820
one that has its problems also.

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:53.010
Are there examples of human collective intelligence
that you can think of where people aren't

00:41:53.010 --> 00:41:58.700
exerting power over each other, where there
is a more genuine form of collectivity?

00:41:58.700 --> 00:41:59.950
Or are there ways in which we're moving toward
that.

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:09.820
I mean, this may relate, Agnieszka, to your
work in providing proceeds from art sales

00:42:09.820 --> 00:42:13.960
to the participates in Amazon Mechanical Turk.

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:24.470
But how is it that we can make a move toward
non-hierarchical types of collective work

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:26.050
among humans?

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:27.623
I think it's an interesting question.

00:42:27.623 --> 00:42:32.350
And one of the reasons why I was fascinated
by this research into artificial societies

00:42:32.350 --> 00:42:36.550
is because this was the first time, I believe,
that modeling very complex behavior of societies

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:43.460
allowed us to understand that any system of
this complexity naturally goes into a disequilibrium.

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:50.860
So even if you give people equal chances on
the start, it will, unless it's monitored

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:51.860
constantly.

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:56.300
And the simple-- in my opinion, the simple
and short answer to your question is no, there

00:42:56.300 --> 00:43:03.900
are no systems like this, because immediately,
unless it's heavily monitored and supervised,

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:11.830
which could entail other problems, such as
surveillance, every system is going to naturally

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:17.490
push into some kind of disequilibrium and
kind of disproportionate relations of power.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:19.900
And I'm obviously exploring.

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:26.440
And that's why I'm looking for like various
weird phenomena that emerge out of the blue

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:34.550
that are really like bottom up emergent processes
that sometimes look strange.

00:43:34.550 --> 00:43:38.770
We don't really know what to make of them,
what is their purpose.

00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:42.810
Some of them almost have a structure of artworks.

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:48.280
Apart from the experiment that I've shown,
there was this experiment, another experiment,

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:51.550
called take care of my plant, which is kind
of absurd.

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:57.000
It's like different people, also in Reddit
platform, different people were asked to participate

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.090
in watering this one plant.

00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:02.950
So they were sending comments through a platform.

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:08.320
And this plant was being nurtured by thousands,
or hundreds of thousands of people.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:09.320
What does this mean?

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:12.280
What is the purpose of it?

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:14.070
It wasn't even a social experiments.

00:44:14.070 --> 00:44:16.060
It was something, some emergent action.

00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:22.630
And I think that the only hope for me is that
looking at spontaneous emergent phenomena

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:29.170
of society, of course including social movements,
but even these very often turn to be hierarchical,

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:33.290
or turn to be-- and there is obviously this
question of headless revolution, whether it's

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:38.300
possible or not, how effective can it be or
not.

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:44.410
But I think this is why it's very interesting
to have this debate with anthropologists,

00:44:44.410 --> 00:44:47.080
because looking into very strange social phenomena
throughout the history of human civilization

00:44:47.080 --> 00:44:50.060
maybe can provide some examples.

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:53.400
But I am rather skeptical.

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:58.080
Could I make a comment on this?

00:44:58.080 --> 00:45:04.230
Because I think the idea of our present models
of artificial intelligence as being somehow

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:06.850
exemplary is deeply flawed.

00:45:06.850 --> 00:45:12.110
Because each of those are based on code that
gets handed on and passed on and is totally

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:15.020
baked into hierarchies of what it was built.

00:45:15.020 --> 00:45:18.780
There's even junk in there that's like [? chat.

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:19.780
?]

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:25.650
So I'm informed by the work of Wendy Chun,
who's a great media theorist, who traced back

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:28.370
the homophily algorithms of Facebook.

00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:31.050
If you like this-- like in Amazon.

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:34.220
If you like this, you'll like this, right?

00:45:34.220 --> 00:45:40.640
The so-called echo chamber that we find ourselves
in socially to a pioneering study in the 1950s

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.460
by an urban theorist.

00:45:42.460 --> 00:45:46.560
And Nick probably knows all of this and remembers
the guy's name.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:52.990
Anyway, it was two paradigms of which only
one was chosen for the future development

00:45:52.990 --> 00:45:54.520
of all sorting algorithms.

00:45:54.520 --> 00:46:00.360
The two paradigms were birds of a feather
live together, sort together.

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:05.310
And the other urban study of neighborhoods
was opposites attract.

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:12.250
These two bits of difference liking and difference
hating sort completely differently.

00:46:12.250 --> 00:46:17.730
And the one that software developers chose
was homophily, was birds of a feather always

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:19.880
want to flock together.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:23.910
That's because they weren't in a dream state.

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:27.590
They couldn't think of the opposite being
this far apart.

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:28.590
It's because they were--

00:46:28.590 --> 00:46:29.590
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:46:29.590 --> 00:46:32.050
--living in the 50s, when redlining was happening
in their neighborhoods.

00:46:32.050 --> 00:46:34.890
They were already whites who were benefiting
from that privilege.

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:36.210
You know what I'm saying?

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:42.450
So even though the urban study guy wanted
to look at the nonracist model, that's not

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:44.860
what got baked into all of our systems.

00:46:44.860 --> 00:46:51.780
So I, as a culturalist and a historian, want
to insist that there is nothing neutral about

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:55.750
the intelligences as we now define them.

00:46:55.750 --> 00:47:02.770
And when I was privileged to be at a session
with Danny Hillis on possible minds with a

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:08.830
bunch of robotics people who now have second
thoughts, mm, what about AI.

00:47:08.830 --> 00:47:10.520
It's scary, actually.

00:47:10.520 --> 00:47:15.020
Anyway, I said, guys, what about the immune
brain?

00:47:15.020 --> 00:47:16.020
You know?

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:17.020
It learns.

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:18.030
It Teaches us.

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:19.120
It remembers things.

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:20.520
It forgets things.

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:22.310
It's distributed.

00:47:22.310 --> 00:47:24.310
It's not exactly conscious, but maybe it is.

00:47:24.310 --> 00:47:25.530
We don't really know.

00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:32.820
The point is it works both to take in and
to reject.

00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:37.930
And the fact that all we spend our money on
is antibiotics and the probiotics aren't even

00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:43.810
regulated by our federal drug authority tells
us something about how our root algorithms

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:45.340
are organized.

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:50.280
So I'm looking for different metaphors to
get going.

00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:54.180
And I'm looking to art for doing that.

00:47:54.180 --> 00:48:03.031
Well, if there's more to say on this, we can,
but there's also other topics to discuss.

00:48:03.031 --> 00:48:04.031
Priorities, power, gender, yeah.

00:48:04.031 --> 00:48:05.031
There's more to say on this.

00:48:05.031 --> 00:48:06.031
There's something--

00:48:06.031 --> 00:48:07.740
Particularly if there's anything hopeful.

00:48:07.740 --> 00:48:15.380
I don't know if I'd add something hopeful,
but I might add something rhizomatic or more

00:48:15.380 --> 00:48:19.810
linky than nodey, in terms of hierarchy setups.

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:26.510
I think that the AI is scary take is interesting.

00:48:26.510 --> 00:48:31.930
I think, also, that the way AI is changing
us in the way that we see technology is a

00:48:31.930 --> 00:48:37.650
sort of a different conversation which gets
left out sometimes in terms of how is it that

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:41.760
we're relating to those sorting algorithms,
and how are they changing us as information

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:43.270
processors and packers.

00:48:43.270 --> 00:48:49.530
Something that I wanted to add, which might
be interesting to the hierarchy conversation,

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:55.480
is transactive memory as a linking concept.

00:48:55.480 --> 00:49:04.760
Meaning culture as a form of collective memory
that no one person holds.

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:10.520
And the notion that I, as an individual in
a culture, don't need to remember, necessarily,

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:14.200
the history of when this space was burned
or built.

00:49:14.200 --> 00:49:17.941
I need to remember the person who remembers
the history.

00:49:17.941 --> 00:49:23.740
And the notion that that sort of linking is
being extended to such a great degree with

00:49:23.740 --> 00:49:30.130
current information and sorting becoming so
effective, where I know-- not only do I not

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:36.420
need to know when the house was built or burned,
I barely need to remember the person who remembers

00:49:36.420 --> 00:49:37.980
the name of the house.

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:44.950
And as you get more effective linking in this
way, I think you start to live more in the

00:49:44.950 --> 00:49:48.150
links rather than in the nodes of information.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:55.080
So I think the interesting thing about it--
in my lab, we do some work on tech and memory.

00:49:55.080 --> 00:50:01.860
And there is really nice work showing that
as you, for instance, teach people about Wikipedia

00:50:01.860 --> 00:50:07.080
pages, they stop remembering that content
and they start remembering how to find the

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:08.080
content.

00:50:08.080 --> 00:50:09.490
They move towards the links.

00:50:09.490 --> 00:50:15.790
I think that I see this in myself in things
like family history, in myself like working

00:50:15.790 --> 00:50:18.130
in a lab with distributed kinds of expertise.

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:24.180
I don't really feel like I need to know much
about circuits, because there's a person next

00:50:24.180 --> 00:50:26.330
door who knows a lot about circuits.

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:33.110
I'm really curious about the kind of hierarchy
that's turning me into.

00:50:33.110 --> 00:50:37.710
And what it means if I live more in the links
to information and less in terms of nodes,

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:42.460
less in terms of expertise, less in terms
of that sort of hierarchy, and what it means

00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:46.470
to start forgetting the things and remembering
the paths to the things.

00:50:46.470 --> 00:50:52.450
Forgetting the name of the thing.

00:50:52.450 --> 00:51:01.670
So, well, let me move on to another question,
to which the answer may also just be no.

00:51:01.670 --> 00:51:06.920
But I'm wondering, because we're characterizing
so many things as collective, we're talking

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:09.620
about the self, the mind.

00:51:09.620 --> 00:51:15.800
Marvin Minsky, of course, had this concept
of society of minds, which makes a metaphor

00:51:15.800 --> 00:51:22.690
to the neural activity and the brain in terms
of society.

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:25.990
Obviously, there's the collective nature of
society.

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:33.560
And there's particular online labor platforms,
there's collaboration also, artistically and

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:35.550
scientifically and otherwise.

00:51:35.550 --> 00:51:39.200
So I guess what I'm wondering is whether it's
useful to distinguish-- is it redundant to

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:40.200
just say collective intelligence?

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:43.910
Is there another kind of intelligence?

00:51:43.910 --> 00:51:50.260
Is there a reason that we would even say that
to put an emphasis on something, what are

00:51:50.260 --> 00:51:53.070
you distinguishing it from?

00:51:53.070 --> 00:52:05.930
I'm not sure if this is also part of the question
that we were discussing earlier about the

00:52:05.930 --> 00:52:09.600
distinction between intelligence and creativity.

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:10.730
I think that these two questions are related.

00:52:10.730 --> 00:52:12.600
And I think that, in principle, in my opinion,
is a false binary.

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:19.000
Because any kind of creativity involves intelligence,
and vise versa.

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:24.270
And a lot of this conversation about creativity
versus intelligence was actually generated

00:52:24.270 --> 00:52:30.240
by neoliberal discourse, Richard Florida,
and exploitation of this creative intelligence

00:52:30.240 --> 00:52:36.900
for purposes of corporations, for example.

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:40.360
And I think that this whole binary is completely
false.

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:47.240
But what, for example, is interesting for
me in terms of collective intelligence, and

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:55.310
why I think that it's important to distinguish
it, even if it's [INAUDIBLE] maybe, is the

00:52:55.310 --> 00:53:01.820
fact that, especially with cultural, but especially
Western culture in particular, has this tradition

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:06.130
of thinking about intelligence as individual.

00:53:06.130 --> 00:53:10.191
And located in the brain and human.

00:53:10.191 --> 00:53:12.640
And I really appreciate what Caroline was
saying.

00:53:12.640 --> 00:53:17.260
I absolutely share your interest in this [? real
?] that this anthropomorphization of intelligence,

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:23.210
and the idea of this individual intelligence,
it has to stop.

00:53:23.210 --> 00:53:27.690
Because not only, obviously, scientists are
observing since a long time, that intelligence

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:28.910
is distributed.

00:53:28.910 --> 00:53:30.110
That it's external to the brain.

00:53:30.110 --> 00:53:32.150
And it can be collective.

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:36.240
It can be also non-human.

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:40.100
And intelligence, essentially, is alien, I
would even say, in a way.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:47.270
And especially with the microbiome research
that is both inside of our bodies, but also

00:53:47.270 --> 00:53:49.560
is external in a way.

00:53:49.560 --> 00:53:52.500
So it's both and non-us.

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:57.580
And I think that it actually brought the whole
conversation-- and I'm coming back to the

00:53:57.580 --> 00:54:06.250
things we said here about AI-- the whole anthropomorphization
of artificial intelligence.

00:54:06.250 --> 00:54:11.280
That it's a single brain, or it's a single
entity, or a robot or something.

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:17.380
It's just like such a blind alley, because
there are actually all these other forms of

00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:24.400
intelligence that-- and in fact, which corresponds
with neural networks, which are used by scientists.

00:54:24.400 --> 00:54:26.110
That it is a multiplicity.

00:54:26.110 --> 00:54:27.470
It's a network.

00:54:27.470 --> 00:54:29.450
It's a [? collective.

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:30.450
?]

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:35.940
So it's useful to ask, who is served by the
fiction of the solitary unit?

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:41.370
And the market is very well served by this.

00:54:41.370 --> 00:54:44.990
So the ICA just opened a beautiful show with
Jason Moran.

00:54:44.990 --> 00:54:50.100
And their stated intention was to sort of
break with the art world's fixation on the

00:54:50.100 --> 00:54:53.690
individual and talk about collaboration.

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:55.990
But it's called Jason Moran.

00:54:55.990 --> 00:54:56.990
You know what I mean?

00:54:56.990 --> 00:55:00.930
So they can't really get there because they're
inside.

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:02.990
They're inside the art market.

00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:05.750
But they know they want to get there, right?

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:11.890
So you can just see the function of these
ideologies as they operate.

00:55:11.890 --> 00:55:17.680
I mean, so one thing you could come to with
the term for lack of intelligence, perhaps

00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:23.140
also like situated or distributed intelligence,
is it doesn't mean that there is another kind.

00:55:23.140 --> 00:55:25.510
It means that we need to explain something
about the nature of intelligence by adding

00:55:25.510 --> 00:55:26.510
these terms.

00:55:26.510 --> 00:55:30.560
We need to adjust our appraisal at the--

00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:38.280
Well, and that, to Adam's point, there's also
the binary with dumb.

00:55:38.280 --> 00:55:40.590
So I mean, we function with that.

00:55:40.590 --> 00:55:46.940
And I'm going to-- Nick didn't want to talk
about this, but MIT has a whole Center for

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:48.710
Collective Intelligence.

00:55:48.710 --> 00:55:53.240
And what they're trying to do over there at
the Sloan School is actually not be dumb,

00:55:53.240 --> 00:55:58.330
not have algorithms determine our president,
for example.

00:55:58.330 --> 00:56:04.300
And that is a very important project, even
though it operates within all the hierarchies,

00:56:04.300 --> 00:56:07.690
with all the biases that could possibly cooked
into all this.

00:56:07.690 --> 00:56:15.370
At least there are some humans that are trying
to move the debate within things that we have

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:21.151
already unleashed, that are already operating
in fairly stupid ways to run our social lives.

00:56:21.151 --> 00:56:27.250
I want everyone to talk about what they want
to talk about [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:27.250 --> 00:56:33.160
I just wanted to quickly-- I think that what
Professor Jones said about moving from emergent

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:35.540
intelligence to emergent being.

00:56:35.540 --> 00:56:39.680
I think it's a really interesting comment.

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:45.540
If smarter is what we're trying to be when
we group together or not.

00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:51.000
There's nice research out of MIT-- I think
it was from [? Malone, ?] and then more recently,

00:56:51.000 --> 00:56:52.000
it's Sandy Pentland.

00:56:52.000 --> 00:56:59.210
And Media Lab doing work on what it is that
predicts effective collaboration.

00:56:59.210 --> 00:57:06.210
It's never picking an individual member to
be smarter than the rest of the group and

00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:08.930
talk more, or even three within 10.

00:57:08.930 --> 00:57:14.140
It's always a measure of social cohesion that's
a best predictor of output.

00:57:14.140 --> 00:57:17.480
And I think that that's research that people
have come across and has been around for a

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:18.480
while.

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:22.000
There's more recent research, which I think
is interesting, in what Professor Jones just

00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:29.240
said about our country making collective decisions
which we'd question, which is that that measure

00:57:29.240 --> 00:57:35.880
of social cohesion is no longer a good measure
of group output if those people are not in

00:57:35.880 --> 00:57:39.600
person, if they're communicating over text
on the internet.

00:57:39.600 --> 00:57:44.960
Then that is no longer the measure that predicts
group intelligence as it was measured in the

00:57:44.960 --> 00:57:46.590
original [? Malone ?] work.

00:57:46.590 --> 00:57:53.600
I find that pretty scary in terms of the ways
that we're going to relate at scale.

00:57:53.600 --> 00:58:00.190
And so I really appreciate the move from emergent
intelligence, emergent being, whether intelligent

00:58:00.190 --> 00:58:03.380
is what we're aiming for, and whether intelligent
is what we're actually studying.

00:58:03.380 --> 00:58:10.150
I think it's really exciting that 100 ants
on a flat surface walk in a circle and die,

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:12.910
and 500,000 can see the world.

00:58:12.910 --> 00:58:15.680
I don't know if what they're doing is being
intelligent.

00:58:15.680 --> 00:58:20.740
I think it's cool to complicate it.

00:58:20.740 --> 00:58:27.100
So let me ask, because the topic has come
up, about authorship, one might say, but also

00:58:27.100 --> 00:58:39.090
being an artist as a singular type of activity,
the myth of the individual author and individual

00:58:39.090 --> 00:58:42.540
artist as something that's promulgated.

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:48.700
I mean copyright law coming about to be able
to make work marketable.

00:58:48.700 --> 00:58:54.550
And of course, the art world has its own version
of this also.

00:58:54.550 --> 00:58:58.690
It's not just in publishing, right?

00:58:58.690 --> 00:59:04.840
So it's actually the case that this work arises,
even if it's not done explicitly by collaborative,

00:59:04.840 --> 00:59:13.040
even if it's not Shakespeare working his plays
with a troop of actors.

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:17.760
Or someone with editorial supervision and
collaboration.

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:20.810
It occurs in the social context.

00:59:20.810 --> 00:59:22.870
It occurs collectively in various ways.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:27.070
And I think we recognize that the standard
individual intelligence, individual creativity

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:28.760
model is an outmoded one.

00:59:28.760 --> 00:59:33.580
But then, I guess, the question is, what's
going to happen with it?

00:59:33.580 --> 00:59:35.490
Is it just going to increasingly be recognized
by the communities that have a stake in this?

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:41.560
Or is it going to be pushed back and further
assertion.

00:59:41.560 --> 00:59:46.270
Where do you see it going in the future?

00:59:46.270 --> 00:59:57.780
Our ethnographer of the future at the end
of the table.

00:59:57.780 --> 01:00:03.860
Well, I think the art world will be the last
to go.

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:11.160
So I'm fascinated by artists who have some
experience with, say, the theatrical arts,

01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:14.250
or the music world, like Jason Moran.

01:00:14.250 --> 01:00:22.230
There are very well elaborated, controlled
systems of credit and sharing in those worlds,

01:00:22.230 --> 01:00:28.170
even down to jazz improvisation, where it's
like it's your turn the drummer.

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:32.920
The second you get into the art world, everything
changes.

01:00:32.920 --> 01:00:39.270
So an artist like William Kentridge, who loves
opera, who wishes he could just do opera,

01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:45.350
needs to go over here and make a drawing and
sell it for $300,000 so he can make an opera,

01:00:45.350 --> 01:00:46.350
right?

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.100
Because opera doesn't make money.

01:00:48.100 --> 01:00:49.900
Opera is collaborative.

01:00:49.900 --> 01:00:56.080
Opera has many, many people working on it,
all of whom get paid and are given credit.

01:00:56.080 --> 01:01:04.340
So ironically, the thing he can do himself
is paid more by the art world than the thing

01:01:04.340 --> 01:01:07.620
that has to pay all those other people.

01:01:07.620 --> 01:01:14.200
That obscures the fact that his workshop has
a stable of very committed people that he

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:21.420
has worked with, almost in a theatrical troupe
impresarial way, for decades.

01:01:21.420 --> 01:01:23.710
And their name does not go on the drawing.

01:01:23.710 --> 01:01:24.710
But let me ask.

01:01:24.710 --> 01:01:25.710
So i understand that from the credit perspective.

01:01:25.710 --> 01:01:27.800
But let me ask this.

01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:34.400
Isn't some of the value, maybe most of the
value, of art that we can talk about it and

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:39.270
we can share our appreciation of and our understanding,
our reflection on it?

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:44.290
And doesn't that come from knowing about artists
and their practices?

01:01:44.290 --> 01:01:50.700
And if we have something-- I mean, Agnieszka's
work is interesting because we know about

01:01:50.700 --> 01:01:56.240
her trajectory as an artist and the contextual
situation of the other works that she's done,

01:01:56.240 --> 01:01:58.730
and her thinking and her perspective and the
way that she works.

01:01:58.730 --> 01:01:59.730
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:01:59.730 --> 01:02:06.540
I think her work is unusual in the amount
of credit that she shares and the discussion

01:02:06.540 --> 01:02:07.650
of her collaborators.

01:02:07.650 --> 01:02:09.450
I think that's unusual.

01:02:09.450 --> 01:02:10.450
And I think that's admirable.

01:02:10.450 --> 01:02:16.710
But we have to admit, we're not as interested
in her Turkers' stories as we are in her--

01:02:16.710 --> 01:02:18.630
But that could be a great hypnagogic reversal.

01:02:18.630 --> 01:02:21.670
That could be a great hypnagogic reversal.

01:02:21.670 --> 01:02:23.400
Instead of averaging those emotions.

01:02:23.400 --> 01:02:25.170
Yes, absolutely.

01:02:25.170 --> 01:02:26.170
Right?

01:02:26.170 --> 01:02:27.830
Drilling down.

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:30.990
And there is some effort to do that.

01:02:30.990 --> 01:02:35.660
There is some work with these affective committees.

01:02:35.660 --> 01:02:40.760
I think-- I'll just throw out another example
of collectives, which I would love to get

01:02:40.760 --> 01:02:42.290
Agnieszka'a response to.

01:02:42.290 --> 01:02:50.920
So in Dafen, which is the special economic
zone of Shenzen, the art village was collected

01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:57.840
by the party state in China and told that
they should each paint their dream on this

01:02:57.840 --> 01:02:58.840
particular canvas.

01:02:58.840 --> 01:03:03.891
But on the other side, they should paint an
exact replica of this thing over here, this

01:03:03.891 --> 01:03:07.190
little chip, this little photographic chip.

01:03:07.190 --> 01:03:12.210
And they're very highly skilled at replicating
photographic information on whatever they're

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:13.210
given.

01:03:13.210 --> 01:03:18.750
And when all of the tiles were put together
by the party state, they made a giant Mona

01:03:18.750 --> 01:03:19.970
Lisa.

01:03:19.970 --> 01:03:25.340
Not from their dreams, which are now on the
back of the canvas, their individual dreams.

01:03:25.340 --> 01:03:32.780
But the front, which they were given as actual
living human mechanical Turkers, now composes

01:03:32.780 --> 01:03:38.430
the Dafen Mona Lisa, which is so much better
than Leonardo's because we all made it together,

01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:39.430
right?

01:03:39.430 --> 01:03:44.250
So this trope of the collective is also a
cultural conceit.

01:03:44.250 --> 01:03:49.860
And it bears with it tremendous power for
the political narrative.

01:03:49.860 --> 01:03:55.760
So the hierarchy that determined which worker
would get which chip, which piece of the Mona

01:03:55.760 --> 01:04:02.130
Lisa, is erased in the discourse about this
wonderful collective.

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:08.110
So you can always do that interesting analysis
of the history and how it works with these

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:10.830
concepts.

01:04:10.830 --> 01:04:17.120
The name, the Mechanical Turk, is an obscene
orientalism that we just use without reflection.

01:04:17.120 --> 01:04:25.490
But it already encodes the hierarchy of this
automaton without brain that will perform

01:04:25.490 --> 01:04:27.140
upon demand.

01:04:27.140 --> 01:04:32.630
I mean, so ugh, can we reverse that narrative
in any way with that platform?

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:36.060
I mean, I think we'd have to rename it.

01:04:36.060 --> 01:04:37.660
Yes, absolutely.

01:04:37.660 --> 01:04:38.660
I mean--

01:04:38.660 --> 01:04:39.660
I'm not sure the other platforms are better,
though.

01:04:39.660 --> 01:04:40.660
Yes.

01:04:40.660 --> 01:04:41.660
There are [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:42.660
[INAUDIBLE] is the other [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:43.660
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:04:43.660 --> 01:04:44.660
So it does seem like historicizing these terms
is one way to break them, right?

01:04:44.660 --> 01:04:51.810
The history of the collective, and collective
farming in the former Soviet Union is a different

01:04:51.810 --> 01:05:00.620
kind of collective than the kind of Utopian
thing that maybe we're talking about a little

01:05:00.620 --> 01:05:01.620
bit here, right?

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:08.050
And I think a lot of discussions of collective
intelligence are-- and emergence intelligence--

01:05:08.050 --> 01:05:11.980
are haunted by kind of reductionist methodological
individualism.

01:05:11.980 --> 01:05:18.810
That somehow there's this collective emanation
of emergent intelligence, and then it's made

01:05:18.810 --> 01:05:25.110
of these binary little bits of individuals,
which is kind of Western, liberal contract

01:05:25.110 --> 01:05:30.570
theory from Hobbes to Russo to John Locke,
et cetera.

01:05:30.570 --> 01:05:34.520
Like mitzy monads that just sort of percolate,
right?

01:05:34.520 --> 01:05:37.540
With no mediating or middle structure.

01:05:37.540 --> 01:05:42.330
But I think that maybe that's something we
could think about, is what would be a meso-theory?

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:49.660
There's something that intervenes between
the kind of monads and this collective effervescence.

01:05:49.660 --> 01:05:51.550
Well, let's go there.

01:05:51.550 --> 01:05:58.830
Because the monad is merely an invagination
or an extrusion from a single divine body.

01:05:58.830 --> 01:06:08.520
So I mean, we could, right there with the
monad, we could get some fuzz and some vagueness

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:11.370
and some sliding going on.

01:06:11.370 --> 01:06:14.510
It's the bit that I think we struggle with.

01:06:14.510 --> 01:06:20.680
I would have two comments to this exchange.

01:06:20.680 --> 01:06:28.190
One is about coming back again to this question
of creativity and different projections and

01:06:28.190 --> 01:06:33.710
anthropomorphizations of AI and its uses.

01:06:33.710 --> 01:06:46.290
And something that is specific, I think, about
intelligence that is not machining is a certain

01:06:46.290 --> 01:06:53.140
aspect of irrationality, or unpredictability,
or uncomputability.

01:06:53.140 --> 01:06:59.850
And I think that the problem that is now starting
to be recognized by scientists, that it's

01:06:59.850 --> 01:07:03.910
this tendency to anthropomorphization, but
also of the AI.

01:07:03.910 --> 01:07:09.380
And trying to think about how this could be
created, and how could we, for example, create

01:07:09.380 --> 01:07:11.210
an artificial intelligence artist using algorithms.

01:07:11.210 --> 01:07:18.740
I mean, one of the questions is like, how
do you actually imitate not the intelligence,

01:07:18.740 --> 01:07:23.680
but precisely the dumbness, the stupidity,
or the thing even more broadly, some kind

01:07:23.680 --> 01:07:27.780
of irrationality of various kinds, including
stupidity?

01:07:27.780 --> 01:07:30.280
Or naivete or-- and this is becoming-- I mean,
it's probably, again, an unbridgeable challenge.

01:07:30.280 --> 01:07:32.200
The machines have their own type of stupidity.

01:07:32.200 --> 01:07:33.400
It's called glitch.

01:07:33.400 --> 01:07:35.010
It's called a glitch.

01:07:35.010 --> 01:07:36.010
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:07:36.010 --> 01:07:39.420
That could be employed in these kind of things.

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:40.420
[INTERPOSING VOICES].

01:07:40.420 --> 01:07:41.740
But they are different glitches.

01:07:41.740 --> 01:07:46.230
They don't have to imitate human writers or
artists.

01:07:46.230 --> 01:07:47.776
Agreed, but there are different kinds of errors
and glitches.

01:07:47.776 --> 01:07:50.910
And of course, some artists are exploring
these glitches, machining glitches.

01:07:50.910 --> 01:07:56.920
But actually, the kind of irrationality, there's
something particular about it.

01:07:56.920 --> 01:08:07.320
And I think that the uncomputability of collective
social behavior, which we can obviously, now,

01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:13.780
with all the algorithms that we have at hand
to, for example, anticipate an election result

01:08:13.780 --> 01:08:15.290
and other phenomenon.

01:08:15.290 --> 01:08:19.970
They've become useless at the end of the day,
because so many irrational factors sometimes

01:08:19.970 --> 01:08:21.220
play into it.

01:08:21.220 --> 01:08:26.950
I think that I see-- I kind of see some hope
in it, because wouldn't it be absolutely terrifying

01:08:26.950 --> 01:08:29.520
if everything could just be computed?

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:36.029
And we would be able to anticipate everything,
like in terms of any kind of creative-- excuse

01:08:36.029 --> 01:08:38.020
the word-- process of any kind.

01:08:38.020 --> 01:08:40.069
Not in culture, but in any.

01:08:40.069 --> 01:08:48.670
I think, actually, this would-- humans need
not only this sense of being able to control

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:54.100
things, because obviously, what is the ability
to calculate things?

01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:58.109
It's obviously connected to our ability to
control things.

01:08:58.109 --> 01:09:03.940
And the more that we have uncomputables and
irrational factors, this actually-- the fact

01:09:03.940 --> 01:09:07.560
that we still can have it, thank God, is a
positive thing.

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:11.839
But I don't know for how long, but I'm hoping
that maybe always.

01:09:11.839 --> 01:09:19.250
And another point that I wanted to make about
the questions of authorship and where art

01:09:19.250 --> 01:09:26.230
and culture could go, I mean, one of my main
areas of research is where is culture going

01:09:26.230 --> 01:09:27.230
to evolve?

01:09:27.230 --> 01:09:28.230
Where it's going.

01:09:28.230 --> 01:09:32.400
And I think that we're living in, for many
reasons, horrifying times.

01:09:32.400 --> 01:09:38.350
But for other reasons, very interesting times,
because we can witness as culture evolves

01:09:38.350 --> 01:09:44.580
in front of our eyes, because some things
are happening so fast.

01:09:44.580 --> 01:09:52.380
But one negative aspect of the evolution of
culture is that it used to be produced collectively.

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:58.810
And things like the Bible, or the mythologies
were important products of culture with multiples

01:09:58.810 --> 01:10:01.100
anonymous, mostly anonymous authors.

01:10:01.100 --> 01:10:07.930
And even today, a lot of people are indicating
that even constructs like Homer, or maybe

01:10:07.930 --> 01:10:13.480
to some degree even Shakespeare, actually
constitute a multiplicity or, or certain diversity

01:10:13.480 --> 01:10:15.900
of more than just one author.

01:10:15.900 --> 01:10:20.610
And at some point of cultural evolution, we
arrived at the point where, basically, yes,

01:10:20.610 --> 01:10:26.860
as Caroline pointed out, cultural industries
or proto-industries capitalized on the fact

01:10:26.860 --> 01:10:31.550
that if you have a singular author, it's going
to be easier to capitalize on it, to market

01:10:31.550 --> 01:10:32.750
it.

01:10:32.750 --> 01:10:40.050
Yeah, and we're like at the point total absurd
of this, that it's a total alienation, almost,

01:10:40.050 --> 01:10:43.040
of the product.

01:10:43.040 --> 01:10:52.610
And I think that creativity is a social relation,
first and foremost.

01:10:52.610 --> 01:10:57.560
And what happened in culture is that creativity
became a relation between the product and

01:10:57.560 --> 01:11:00.110
the author.

01:11:00.110 --> 01:11:09.570
And this is why, precisely as Caroline was
pointing out, that the objects that were touched

01:11:09.570 --> 01:11:17.120
by the artist hand-- why is painting today,
as for example, like Isabella Grav very interestingly

01:11:17.120 --> 01:11:25.580
analyzes, like why has painting been so fetishized
and is reaching the highest prices is because

01:11:25.580 --> 01:11:30.840
a lot of people who understand culture in
a very basic way, they want to have the stigma

01:11:30.840 --> 01:11:35.020
of the artist, the touch of the artist's hand,
even if it's mechanized to some degree.

01:11:35.020 --> 01:11:40.190
But still, some kind of expanded notion of
painting, or expanded notion of sculpture.

01:11:40.190 --> 01:11:44.290
Something that had a contact with the living
body of the artist.

01:11:44.290 --> 01:11:52.540
And it's obviously just another commodification
of a process that originally was general,

01:11:52.540 --> 01:11:55.040
intellect, multitude of various people.

01:11:55.040 --> 01:12:02.030
Let me ask, though, because it seems to me
that it is possible, if you're mentioning

01:12:02.030 --> 01:12:09.670
with regard to Homer, the Odyssey author,
the Iliad author-- we call him Homer.

01:12:09.670 --> 01:12:15.260
That we can have works that don't have an
author, and that are creative, and influential

01:12:15.260 --> 01:12:16.260
socially.

01:12:16.260 --> 01:12:23.980
But we can't have something creative or influential
socially without some type of cultural context.

01:12:23.980 --> 01:12:31.420
We wouldn't say, oh, this is very creative
meteorite that landed on the ground.

01:12:31.420 --> 01:12:37.070
I mean, it has to be a work that has some
meaning situated socially that's there.

01:12:37.070 --> 01:12:38.250
And so--

01:12:38.250 --> 01:12:43.360
I don't know, it it looked like Agnieszka's
sculpture, we might say, what a creative looking

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:44.360
meteorite.

01:12:44.360 --> 01:12:45.360
That's true.

01:12:45.360 --> 01:12:46.360
Yes.

01:12:46.360 --> 01:12:51.480
In other words, if it had certain orders that
we associate-- But there was also the selection

01:12:51.480 --> 01:12:52.480
of materials.

01:12:52.480 --> 01:12:56.330
There was also an arrangement of--

01:12:56.330 --> 01:13:01.250
But upon first contact, if it rockets out
of the sky, it lands in front of you, and

01:13:01.250 --> 01:13:05.440
it looks like her anodized metal thing--

01:13:05.440 --> 01:13:06.840
This is an empirical question.

01:13:06.840 --> 01:13:13.670
If we go to the Museum of Natural History
and we take one of the meteorites and we put

01:13:13.670 --> 01:13:14.670
it in an art gallery--

01:13:14.670 --> 01:13:16.520
Oh, it's been.

01:13:16.520 --> 01:13:19.670
Olafur Eliasson already did that.

01:13:19.670 --> 01:13:24.480
But I'm making a different point about where
culture comes from.

01:13:24.480 --> 01:13:27.690
So if it rockets out of the sky, it lands
in front of us, and it looks like Agnieszka's

01:13:27.690 --> 01:13:32.840
object, I'm going to say there's probably
some culture in this.

01:13:32.840 --> 01:13:39.230
So I'm supplying-- I'm part of the-- what
I love, what Adam mentioned, about this collective

01:13:39.230 --> 01:13:44.360
project that defers memory and stores it in
other people, and so on and so forth.

01:13:44.360 --> 01:13:50.920
I mean, maybe it would be a provocative suggestion
to say, are we allowing corporations to have

01:13:50.920 --> 01:13:52.130
collective intelligence?

01:13:52.130 --> 01:13:53.540
They're already fictive persons.

01:13:53.540 --> 01:13:57.730
They're already fictive bodies.

01:13:57.730 --> 01:14:04.400
What are we-- we have many, many entities
in our own culture that are kind of allowed

01:14:04.400 --> 01:14:08.690
to operate as if they possessed intelligence.

01:14:08.690 --> 01:14:15.770
And some of the AI crowd I was hanging out
with was saying that this is already the singularity.

01:14:15.770 --> 01:14:18.910
This is already an artificial intelligence.

01:14:18.910 --> 01:14:24.980
Their claim is that the CEO has to listen
to this aggregate that is this corporation.

01:14:24.980 --> 01:14:33.210
That the CEO is a fictive individual who doesn't
actually control what's going on with this

01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:37.730
emergent property which is called the capitalist
corporation.

01:14:37.730 --> 01:14:41.540
So I take that pretty seriously.

01:14:41.540 --> 01:14:45.180
There's a history to that.

01:14:45.180 --> 01:14:46.920
We decided to make them people.

01:14:46.920 --> 01:14:49.020
We decided to let them have free speech.

01:14:49.020 --> 01:14:55.970
We decided to let have certain ways of storing
their capital and moving it around that didn't

01:14:55.970 --> 01:14:58.880
involve our separate wishes.

01:14:58.880 --> 01:15:06.420
So some have argued that this is already the
singularity of a general intelligence appearing

01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:07.720
in our social organizations.

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:08.720
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:15:08.720 --> 01:15:13.030
--language and have conversations with them
to ask them to treat us nicely.

01:15:13.030 --> 01:15:15.210
I think that's called a chat room.

01:15:15.210 --> 01:15:16.840
I think I do that on my interfaces.

01:15:16.840 --> 01:15:23.360
I'm also thinking back to the 19th century
and Charles Babbage's notion of intelligence

01:15:23.360 --> 01:15:31.780
as not only a kind of capacity or a faculty,
but also as knowledge about the conduct of

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:32.790
calculation.

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:37.740
And Charles Babbage not only being famous
for thinking about early theory in computation,

01:15:37.740 --> 01:15:42.760
but also somebody who is very interested in
factory manufacture, and in organizing the

01:15:42.760 --> 01:15:49.640
shop floor, and making the claim that having
the knowledge about how to organize the shop

01:15:49.640 --> 01:15:52.330
floor meant having intelligence.

01:15:52.330 --> 01:15:57.560
And the intelligence resided not in the craftspeople
or in the factory workers on the floor, but

01:15:57.560 --> 01:15:58.560
rather in the manager.

01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:04.080
So already there, there's a notion of-- there's
already a kind of relation of production that

01:16:04.080 --> 01:16:07.320
produces a kind of notion of what counts as
intelligence.

01:16:07.320 --> 01:16:14.270
And it's gathering together of a collectivity
into the category of the stake of the individual.

01:16:14.270 --> 01:16:19.650
I just want to quickly to the point of irrationality
and authorship in the corporation.

01:16:19.650 --> 01:16:26.810
I think another institution project director
that's at the table and in the room, the sort

01:16:26.810 --> 01:16:30.680
of collective scientist and the collection
of facts that science produces.

01:16:30.680 --> 01:16:35.450
And the notion of authorship in that realm
I think is really interesting.

01:16:35.450 --> 01:16:40.680
In my experience, there's almost an opposing
problem, which is that-- in my experience,

01:16:40.680 --> 01:16:44.000
the art world, art rarely exist without an
author.

01:16:44.000 --> 01:16:47.940
In my experience of science and scientific
discussions about how memory does or doesn't

01:16:47.940 --> 01:16:53.700
work, or is split or not split, or stays or
doesn't stay, facts are split from their producers.

01:16:53.700 --> 01:17:00.420
And I think this is interesting to Professor
Jones' point in terms of power in code also,

01:17:00.420 --> 01:17:05.290
the notion of math or software as neutral
and without author.

01:17:05.290 --> 01:17:08.520
And then in the science world, the notion
of facts as without author.

01:17:08.520 --> 01:17:12.420
And what that produced is-- and why that comforts
us.

01:17:12.420 --> 01:17:17.070
I think it eliminates subjects and the limitations
of specific cohorts of subjects.

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:19.380
It eliminates the scientist as subjective.

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:24.150
It eliminates the study of myself as study
of other, which I think is comforting as a

01:17:24.150 --> 01:17:28.180
construction of objectivity and a way to study
ourselves or know ourselves, actually.

01:17:28.180 --> 01:17:34.700
But I think in the same way, there's an interesting
psychology of the artist as always author,

01:17:34.700 --> 01:17:39.290
or science as without author, sort of exiting
[INAUDIBLE] science.

01:17:39.290 --> 01:17:44.790
There is an interesting psychology to why
I think it's comforting to consider myself,

01:17:44.790 --> 01:17:52.520
or ourselves, or intelligence as a collective
in that it lets you understand and not understand

01:17:52.520 --> 01:17:53.520
at once.

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:54.540
And that's OK.

01:17:54.540 --> 01:17:58.520
Where you can say, you can boil down to parts,
and I can see parts.

01:17:58.520 --> 01:18:00.930
And so I'm a materialist scientist, and that
is comforting.

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:05.700
But at the same time, there's a sort of weak,
epic phenomenalism where you can say, those

01:18:05.700 --> 01:18:07.470
parts construct a mystery.

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:12.600
And that leaves some space for freewill, or
soul, or whatever you'd like to put in there.

01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:15.430
So I think the psychologies of all of these
are really interesting.

01:18:15.430 --> 01:18:20.630
The psychology of giving personhood to corporation.

01:18:20.630 --> 01:18:25.150
The psychology of giving, perhaps, too much
personhood to an art project, or perhaps taking

01:18:25.150 --> 01:18:28.910
personhood out of a scientific project.

01:18:28.910 --> 01:18:36.050
Well, I think we should break the hermetic
seal around the five of us and see if there's

01:18:36.050 --> 01:18:41.640
some questions from our continuing audience
that's been so kind to [INAUDIBLE].

01:18:41.640 --> 01:18:43.760
We'll be voting them up and down over here.

01:18:43.760 --> 01:18:48.270
We'll be voting your questions up.

01:18:48.270 --> 01:18:54.540
Let's-- yes.

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:57.670
Hi.

01:18:57.670 --> 01:19:07.600
I'm at the MIT Center for Collective Intelligence.

01:19:07.600 --> 01:19:08.600
I have a lot of questions, comments, [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:08.600 --> 01:19:09.600
I told that this was coming.

01:19:09.600 --> 01:19:12.410
The one that I believe-- it's not clear to
me yet how this panel defines intelligence.

01:19:12.410 --> 01:19:13.410
Let's be pushy.

01:19:13.410 --> 01:19:21.210
One of the things that I was wondering is,
if intelligence and creativity would be considered

01:19:21.210 --> 01:19:26.040
distinctly, as some people, for instance,
in computational creativity like to think

01:19:26.040 --> 01:19:31.080
of creativity as being distinct from artificial
intelligence the way that problem solving

01:19:31.080 --> 01:19:32.860
behavior is treated there.

01:19:32.860 --> 01:19:38.340
So Agnieszka's perspective was not-- was that
they shouldn't be though of separately.

01:19:38.340 --> 01:19:43.190
What's intelligence to you?

01:19:43.190 --> 01:19:45.690
I'm going to defer to the ethnographer.

01:19:45.690 --> 01:19:48.360
I'm interested in the actors categories.

01:19:48.360 --> 01:19:53.433
So my main question with the slime mold is,
what is this thing that you mean when you

01:19:53.433 --> 01:19:55.900
say the word intelligence?

01:19:55.900 --> 01:20:31.610
So mostly, I want to ask that question, not
answer it.

01:20:31.610 --> 01:20:33.230
I actually like what you presented there,
that example, a single episodes And the way

01:20:33.230 --> 01:20:34.230
that you actually described it as a collective
aggregation instead of collective intelligence

01:20:34.230 --> 01:20:35.230
I didn't see intelligence there.

01:20:35.230 --> 01:20:36.230
But I was able to see this collective But
that's based on my perception and understanding

01:20:36.230 --> 01:20:37.230
of what intelligence means.

01:20:37.230 --> 01:20:38.230
But I was very interested to hear what your
perspective was.

01:20:38.230 --> 01:20:39.550
So I think that the emerging ideas of art
and culture that I see as celebrating in some

01:20:39.550 --> 01:20:48.290
way, as some demi hemi semi collective up
here, is to question the computational motherboard

01:20:48.290 --> 01:20:50.750
as the metaphor for intelligence.

01:20:50.750 --> 01:21:00.860
The baggage manager, the man behind the curtain,
the [INAUDIBLE] or whatever [? Wiener ?] call

01:21:00.860 --> 01:21:03.210
it.

01:21:03.210 --> 01:21:09.020
The kind of gubernator, the cybernetic steersman
to kind of question that.

01:21:09.020 --> 01:21:14.290
And to say that it's time to acknowledge that
other things are happening, things that seem

01:21:14.290 --> 01:21:23.310
to have purpose and form and seem to push
things into being that are not necessarily

01:21:23.310 --> 01:21:27.770
driven by some rational intelligence at the
center.

01:21:27.770 --> 01:21:31.960
So do we want to abandon the word, or do we
want to retool it?

01:21:31.960 --> 01:21:34.190
That's kind of the project.

01:21:34.190 --> 01:21:35.330
That's the question at hand.

01:21:35.330 --> 01:21:39.170
I'm not sure which is-- maybe it's fatally
compromised.

01:21:39.170 --> 01:21:43.310
It certainly has its sociological and racist
baggage.

01:21:43.310 --> 01:21:45.170
So maybe we--

01:21:45.170 --> 01:21:48.960
But again, there's a market there as well,
right?

01:21:48.960 --> 01:21:59.510
When I wanted to get some digitized images
for teaching, I got nowhere with the library.

01:21:59.510 --> 01:22:00.960
They didn't have any money.

01:22:00.960 --> 01:22:04.290
But when I called it an IS&amp;T problem, and
I called it an information management problem,

01:22:04.290 --> 01:22:09.770
suddenly I had all these people saying, we'll
write some software for that.

01:22:09.770 --> 01:22:16.530
So they thought they could sell an intelligent
system of digital-- like they kind of forgot

01:22:16.530 --> 01:22:17.530
PowerPoint existed.

01:22:17.530 --> 01:22:20.560
But they thought they could sell an intelligent
teaching platform.

01:22:20.560 --> 01:22:23.320
The library didn't have anything to sell.

01:22:23.320 --> 01:22:25.530
They just had all of this collective memory.

01:22:25.530 --> 01:22:28.970
MIT gives it a budget.

01:22:28.970 --> 01:22:33.130
So this is-- you know, there's also a market
in intelligence.

01:22:33.130 --> 01:22:39.980
I think, for one thing, that your definition
is contextually suggested.

01:22:39.980 --> 01:22:45.440
So in many ways, at MIT, what intelligence
is is this sort of back formation from artificial

01:22:45.440 --> 01:22:47.790
intelligence.

01:22:47.790 --> 01:22:48.810
And if we were--

01:22:48.810 --> 01:22:50.980
And a back formation there from cybernetics--

01:22:50.980 --> 01:22:51.980
If we were in government--

01:22:51.980 --> 01:22:53.210
--which were military guidance systems.

01:22:53.210 --> 01:22:56.150
But if we were in government and political
science, we would think that intelligence

01:22:56.150 --> 01:23:02.200
maybe was gathering satellite and human intelligence
data from places and trying to integrate it

01:23:02.200 --> 01:23:05.310
to learn things about other countries and
actors.

01:23:05.310 --> 01:23:11.050
And at MIT, it's also a techno-scientific
category, whereas that's the tool with which--

01:23:11.050 --> 01:23:14.120
that's the discourse with which to approach
it, right?

01:23:14.120 --> 01:23:16.210
Rather than intelligent design.

01:23:16.210 --> 01:23:20.970
Rather than with the tools of a particular
religious cosmology.

01:23:20.970 --> 01:23:30.790
That's why I'm trying to push the conversation
within my work, my questions I'm asking myself,

01:23:30.790 --> 01:23:37.420
as far away from any anthropomorphization,
human associated.

01:23:37.420 --> 01:23:42.980
But also machining into other realms, into
[? alien ?] intelligence, into microbial intelligence,

01:23:42.980 --> 01:23:45.820
into non-organic intelligence, like intelligence
of particles.

01:23:45.820 --> 01:23:54.860
Because I think that one of the problems is
that we associated all this.

01:23:54.860 --> 01:24:02.500
And I think a lot of these notions were exactly
created on the basis of certain cliches that

01:24:02.500 --> 01:24:05.560
emerged in various fields.

01:24:05.560 --> 01:24:06.930
And we have to take it somewhere.

01:24:06.930 --> 01:24:14.680
And for me, in one word, like the intelligence,
like if really something is the unknown.

01:24:14.680 --> 01:24:22.980
Something that is-- I'm looking for it in
the unknown, the irrational, the other worldly.

01:24:22.980 --> 01:24:30.250
And because all these other categories we've
kind of, I fell, almost like hit the wall.

01:24:30.250 --> 01:24:31.250
And it's just--

01:24:31.250 --> 01:24:32.250
To go back to--

01:24:32.250 --> 01:24:33.250
Maybe-- I was going to see if we could have
maybe one or two more questions.

01:24:33.250 --> 01:24:34.250
But is it.

01:24:34.250 --> 01:24:35.250
There was one in back, yes.

01:24:35.250 --> 01:24:36.970
I'm also [INAUDIBLE] Apart from [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:36.970 --> 01:24:37.970
I'm sorry.

01:24:37.970 --> 01:24:54.130
I didn't-- can you rephrase the question?

01:24:54.130 --> 01:24:57.940
It's a question as to whether we have new
discoveries on slime mold,s which I must admit,

01:24:57.940 --> 01:24:58.940
I do not.

01:24:58.940 --> 01:25:01.481
I have some.

01:25:01.481 --> 01:25:02.481
But Agnieszka may be able to fill us in.

01:25:02.481 --> 01:25:07.280
I don't know if maybe you came up late to
the talk, but I was showing that image of

01:25:07.280 --> 01:25:08.490
slime mold solving a maze.

01:25:08.490 --> 01:25:13.570
Most recently, what was established about
the slime mold is that it has memory.

01:25:13.570 --> 01:25:15.450
It's capable of collectively memorizing.

01:25:15.450 --> 01:25:21.990
Which kind of also refers to the question
about intelligence, because, of course, remembering

01:25:21.990 --> 01:25:22.990
and learning.

01:25:22.990 --> 01:25:27.000
It has a capability of learning, even though
it doesn't have a nervous system.

01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:28.730
So it's, in a way, similar to a neural network.

01:25:28.730 --> 01:25:33.780
That it somehow learns.

01:25:33.780 --> 01:25:40.140
I have a book to recommend, which is-- there's
a professor at Princeton who has spent his

01:25:40.140 --> 01:25:45.230
life with these organisms, kind of along the
model of the wonderful Japanese researcher

01:25:45.230 --> 01:25:51.280
who presented them to the emperor in 1930
as the model of a good society.

01:25:51.280 --> 01:25:54.220
Anyway, the guy at Princeton's last name is
Bonner.

01:25:54.220 --> 01:25:56.430
And the book is just called The Social Ameobae.

01:25:56.430 --> 01:26:01.260
And it's beautifully illustrated, and it's
small.

01:26:01.260 --> 01:26:02.980
It's lovely to read.

01:26:02.980 --> 01:26:09.720
And he talks about all the things that are
known and the much larger things that aren't

01:26:09.720 --> 01:26:13.080
known about how this creature organizes.

01:26:13.080 --> 01:26:18.890
And it's in the family of fungi, right?

01:26:18.890 --> 01:26:21.450
We don't know much about fungi.

01:26:21.450 --> 01:26:23.240
They are everywhere.

01:26:23.240 --> 01:26:32.210
And they are-- the plants are down here in
the evolutionary line.

01:26:32.210 --> 01:26:34.270
Animals are here in the evolutionary line.

01:26:34.270 --> 01:26:37.440
And fungi are-- they're kind of right up there.

01:26:37.440 --> 01:26:38.860
They act collaboratively.

01:26:38.860 --> 01:26:44.660
They provide communication channels for other
symbiotic creatures that they're involved

01:26:44.660 --> 01:26:45.690
with-- trees.

01:26:45.690 --> 01:26:53.980
I mean, so stay tuned, because the fungi are
giving us a lot of the chemical signalling

01:26:53.980 --> 01:26:56.520
that is just beginning to be seen as intelligent.

01:26:56.520 --> 01:26:57.640
I'm with you on [INAUDIBLE].

01:26:57.640 --> 01:27:02.280
I want to ask, though, about the idea that
repetition of previous states and shapes,

01:27:02.280 --> 01:27:04.560
in that we call this memory.

01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:09.070
I mean, we also have a memory foam pillow
that does this.

01:27:09.070 --> 01:27:11.620
And we wouldn't attribute it with the intelligence.

01:27:11.620 --> 01:27:16.550
In fact, the fact that our computers have
memory is not the way that Babbage, of course,

01:27:16.550 --> 01:27:17.550
talked of them.

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:21.070
He talked of not the processor and memory,
but the mill and the store.

01:27:21.070 --> 01:27:24.630
So he had the industrial metaphor for computing.

01:27:24.630 --> 01:27:31.630
And our computer would be spoken of much more
like in these factory terms, except for the

01:27:31.630 --> 01:27:36.050
fact that it was later reframed as a giant
brain.

01:27:36.050 --> 01:27:39.810
Right, but we have gigabytes on storage.

01:27:39.810 --> 01:27:42.560
So the store-- the store does get there.

01:27:42.560 --> 01:27:44.510
I mean, these aren't the only metaphors.

01:27:44.510 --> 01:27:50.490
But I'm saying that we often project into
these concepts metaphorically.

01:27:50.490 --> 01:27:55.170
And so-- not that slime molds aren't interesting.

01:27:55.170 --> 01:27:59.290
I'm not trying to assert that in this crowd,
definitely.

01:27:59.290 --> 01:28:05.380
But we should also be thinking about what
we are figuratively discussing and what we're

01:28:05.380 --> 01:28:06.800
projecting onto them by having these different
concepts.

01:28:06.800 --> 01:28:09.730
I mean, the social-- the name social, what
does that-- I mean, does that make any sense?

01:28:09.730 --> 01:28:10.730
No.

01:28:10.730 --> 01:28:11.730
Who knows?

01:28:11.730 --> 01:28:12.730
Is there-- yes.

01:28:12.730 --> 01:28:13.730
Yeah, it seems like some of the ways we have
right now of acknowledging and respecting

01:28:13.730 --> 01:28:14.730
the sort of collective intelligence, or this
act of producing something collectively is

01:28:14.730 --> 01:28:15.730
like profit sharing and credit sharing.

01:28:15.730 --> 01:28:16.730
I think along those lines, I feel like thinking
about the cases of acting in a collective

01:28:16.730 --> 01:28:17.730
with termites, or acting in a collective with
artificial intelligence kind of shows the

01:28:17.730 --> 01:28:18.730
limits of approaching it that way.

01:28:18.730 --> 01:28:34.680
So what might it look like to really-- what's
the equivalent of profit sharing with artificial

01:28:34.680 --> 01:28:38.500
intelligence or with termites?

01:28:38.500 --> 01:28:46.700
I'm not sure I understood the question.

01:28:46.700 --> 01:29:03.670
What is the equivalent of profit sharing with
termites?

01:29:03.670 --> 01:29:04.670
What's in it for the termites?

01:29:04.670 --> 01:29:05.750
Yeah, what's it in for the termites, yeah.

01:29:05.750 --> 01:29:07.910
What's in it for the termites?

01:29:07.910 --> 01:29:08.910
Yes.

01:29:08.910 --> 01:29:15.130
Well actually, I have to say, honestly, I
consciously used the termites as a model of

01:29:15.130 --> 01:29:21.770
self-exploitation, because the termites, they
don't-- this is an interesting phenomenon

01:29:21.770 --> 01:29:23.600
that they are almost blind.

01:29:23.600 --> 01:29:26.280
They don't see what they're building out of,
for example.

01:29:26.280 --> 01:29:31.250
They don't see that these are these crazy
colors, instead of like the gray or orange

01:29:31.250 --> 01:29:32.830
or black mud.

01:29:32.830 --> 01:29:36.750
And it was just like a really, for me, interesting
vehicle to talk about self-exploitations.

01:29:36.750 --> 01:29:40.840
It's like, how our data is exploited, or like
how--

01:29:40.840 --> 01:29:41.840
I have to point out, they weren't even invited
to the opening.

01:29:41.840 --> 01:29:42.840
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:42.840 --> 01:29:48.420
But to kind of develop further on your question,
obviously-- a question that I'm being asked

01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:52.250
very often is OK, so you're talking about
this collective intelligence, and a collective

01:29:52.250 --> 01:30:01.550
authorship, but at the end of the day, you're
just like a single author.

01:30:01.550 --> 01:30:08.440
And one of the things that interests me, I
think that these questions of singular authorship,

01:30:08.440 --> 01:30:12.200
we're really witnessing some sort of erosion
of the singular author.

01:30:12.200 --> 01:30:13.710
But it's not that we're already-- I don't
really know.

01:30:13.710 --> 01:30:15.690
This is one of my areas of inquiry.

01:30:15.690 --> 01:30:18.000
Where is it going to go?

01:30:18.000 --> 01:30:25.780
I just know that individual authorship is
kind of eroding.

01:30:25.780 --> 01:30:27.040
And we can see it already in science.

01:30:27.040 --> 01:30:29.610
We can see it in knowledge production.

01:30:29.610 --> 01:30:35.520
In individual arts, as Caroline pointed out,
they will be the last to go, absolutely.

01:30:35.520 --> 01:30:36.890
I couldn't agree more.

01:30:36.890 --> 01:30:43.550
But for example, when you look at science,
how Nobel Prize used to be just given to a

01:30:43.550 --> 01:30:44.550
single person.

01:30:44.550 --> 01:30:46.880
More recently, there's a kind of uptick there.

01:30:46.880 --> 01:30:49.860
Because more recently, not only is given to
groups of people.

01:30:49.860 --> 01:30:52.960
Sometimes a team of two, three, four scientists.

01:30:52.960 --> 01:30:59.920
But also, more interestingly, it's given simultaneously
to two or three people who discovered the

01:30:59.920 --> 01:31:02.260
same thing in different parts of the world.

01:31:02.260 --> 01:31:07.530
And I think that this is an interesting development
that may lead to something else, and also

01:31:07.530 --> 01:31:09.030
in the field of culture.

01:31:09.030 --> 01:31:14.240
But we may not be witnessing this.

01:31:14.240 --> 01:31:16.170
I just think that something is changing.

01:31:16.170 --> 01:31:18.040
And I'm happy to be witnessing this.

01:31:18.040 --> 01:31:20.880
But where it's going to go, I don't know.

01:31:20.880 --> 01:31:29.190
And I want to just add that if you want to
see the models of profit on the animal kingdoms

01:31:29.190 --> 01:31:34.040
and the fungal kingdoms, I mean, brewer's
yeast was the first animal to be-- I mean,

01:31:34.040 --> 01:31:37.340
the first fungi to be sequenced.

01:31:37.340 --> 01:31:41.570
It is the origin of the word biotechnology,
the brewer's yeast.

01:31:41.570 --> 01:31:46.570
So we've been exploiting these branches for
a long, long time.

01:31:46.570 --> 01:31:50.020
And what's interesting is that humans reflect
on these practices.

01:31:50.020 --> 01:31:57.270
So humans are saying, well, it was fine to
have a couple of pigs, but 7,000 in a windowless

01:31:57.270 --> 01:31:59.950
container, is that fine?

01:31:59.950 --> 01:32:04.670
So in my own work, I'm interested in these
modes of visibilization, and the fact that

01:32:04.670 --> 01:32:14.070
we create architectures with no windows because
some part of our culture is nervous that the

01:32:14.070 --> 01:32:17.340
question, what's in it for the pigs, is going
to come up.

01:32:17.340 --> 01:32:24.070
When we're just-- we've engineered them to
have six months lifespans where they triple

01:32:24.070 --> 01:32:26.860
in size on particular kinds of feed.

01:32:26.860 --> 01:32:31.950
I mean, we're producing them as machines to
feed us.

01:32:31.950 --> 01:32:40.230
And so the question of ethics of these relationships,
it's being addressed, but in very personalized

01:32:40.230 --> 01:32:42.320
ways that fit with the neoliberalism.

01:32:42.320 --> 01:32:45.250
Did you eat a hamburger today?

01:32:45.250 --> 01:32:51.110
Rather than what is our collective responsibility
to this planet and to the life forms?

01:32:51.110 --> 01:32:52.600
Should we always maximize human life?

01:32:52.600 --> 01:32:56.320
I mean, is that part of the co-organization?

01:32:56.320 --> 01:33:04.591
So these questions of the collective are still
pressing and make me want to cry, because

01:33:04.591 --> 01:33:09.920
we need to have these conversations all the
time.

01:33:09.920 --> 01:33:11.600
We need to talk.

01:33:11.600 --> 01:33:12.600
So I really value your question.

01:33:12.600 --> 01:33:13.600
Well, I do as well.

01:33:13.600 --> 01:33:16.720
Caroline, I think that one of the things about
it is that it also brings the question down

01:33:16.720 --> 01:33:21.010
to the specifics of, how do we collaborate?

01:33:21.010 --> 01:33:28.710
Not how do we solve every global problem related
to industrial agriculture and ecological disaster

01:33:28.710 --> 01:33:30.590
and so on.

01:33:30.590 --> 01:33:37.130
But how is it that we, in our own practices,
could be fairer to crowdsourced labor that

01:33:37.130 --> 01:33:38.210
we employ.

01:33:38.210 --> 01:33:45.270
But how do we think about this in relationship
to other sort of machine, animal, unusual

01:33:45.270 --> 01:33:46.270
collaborations?

01:33:46.270 --> 01:33:54.440
But how do we situate that in what we might
do as people working in art science, or as

01:33:54.440 --> 01:33:55.530
others thinking about those types of collaborations?

01:33:55.530 --> 01:33:56.530
Yes.

01:33:56.530 --> 01:33:57.530
So we kind of gave this horrible perversion
of the [INAUDIBLE] challenging the way you

01:33:57.530 --> 01:33:58.530
purchase things from the Amazon or the eBay.

01:33:58.530 --> 01:34:00.460
So I know that Jeff Bezos son goes here, so
if I were to bump into him, what would you

01:34:00.460 --> 01:34:06.030
recommend I pass along as a renaming Mechanical
Turk?

01:34:06.030 --> 01:34:07.510
[LAUGHTER]

01:34:07.510 --> 01:34:11.940
I don't know.

01:34:11.940 --> 01:34:14.900
Distributed automaton.

01:34:14.900 --> 01:34:19.330
I don't know.

01:34:19.330 --> 01:34:26.730
Do you have an idea?

01:34:26.730 --> 01:34:34.670
I'm pretty excited in terms of the power of
reframing these different metaphors for work

01:34:34.670 --> 01:34:39.740
and these different metaphors for intelligence.

01:34:39.740 --> 01:34:42.910
And how that lets you reframe the person who
asks the questions.

01:34:42.910 --> 01:34:46.910
I'm super excited about fungal networks and
mushrooms.

01:34:46.910 --> 01:34:53.170
I would probably go ask-- if you come across
Paul Stamets, but he lectures on mushrooms

01:34:53.170 --> 01:34:55.080
while wearing a mushroom as a hat.

01:34:55.080 --> 01:34:56.080
And is an inspired creator.

01:34:56.080 --> 01:35:02.400
I would probably knock on his door and ask
what he means by intelligence.

01:35:02.400 --> 01:35:09.100
A sort of intelligence which makes up the
world's largest and the world's oldest organism,

01:35:09.100 --> 01:35:14.090
and which forms a sort of intelligence that
creates a forest over hundreds of years I

01:35:14.090 --> 01:35:22.560
think offers a lot of challenges to that sort
of single author versus collective force question.

01:35:22.560 --> 01:35:28.210
It offers a lot of challenges to the short
term profit versus long term world question.

01:35:28.210 --> 01:35:29.550
So I'd add mushrooms.

01:35:29.550 --> 01:35:32.240
There's some good mushroom names, puffballs.

01:35:32.240 --> 01:35:37.170
But you're asking about different kinds of
framings and language, right?

01:35:37.170 --> 01:35:40.310
So people do use the word clicklabor.

01:35:40.310 --> 01:35:43.000
But what are some other terms?

01:35:43.000 --> 01:35:44.000
Click bait.

01:35:44.000 --> 01:35:51.500
I mean, maybe there would be a way to name
it that-- I don't know how exploited that

01:35:51.500 --> 01:35:52.500
it is.

01:35:52.500 --> 01:35:57.770
So somebody is working for IBM and they're
bored, so they go over here and make a dollar.

01:35:57.770 --> 01:35:59.330
I mean, is it terribly exploitative?

01:35:59.330 --> 01:36:05.860
I mean, it's far worse how Apple is polishing
its cases in China, the toxic chemicals.

01:36:05.860 --> 01:36:13.470
So maybe there's a name for it, where maybe
we would call it distracted cognitive capital.

01:36:13.470 --> 01:36:17.130
Distracted cognitive capitalism

01:36:17.130 --> 01:36:18.340
[LAUGHTER]

01:36:18.340 --> 01:36:19.340
That sounds wonderful.

01:36:19.340 --> 01:36:24.070
Time you ripped off from your boss, when it
looked like you were working, or something

01:36:24.070 --> 01:36:25.070
like that.

01:36:25.070 --> 01:36:26.120
I mean, maybe there's be a way to name it--

01:36:26.120 --> 01:36:29.770
You should ask, what Joseph Weizenbaum would
say about this is what I think.

01:36:29.770 --> 01:36:40.800
The idea that framing human activity in computation
terms, and putting this type of-- essentially,

01:36:40.800 --> 01:36:47.670
stuffing people into the automaton of the
Mechanical Turk.

01:36:47.670 --> 01:36:51.940
It's not so much that-- it's not the reference
to Turkey that's the problem there.

01:36:51.940 --> 01:36:56.660
It's actually a deeper problem of how the
project is formulated, that right now, we're

01:36:56.660 --> 01:37:01.100
using people to do some tasks that happen
to be part of what computers can do.

01:37:01.100 --> 01:37:06.790
As soon as we get that figured out, we won't
have to deal with that.

01:37:06.790 --> 01:37:07.790
And so--

01:37:07.790 --> 01:37:15.910
I do want to point out that the giant conversation
could be what about the three day workweek?

01:37:15.910 --> 01:37:18.840
What about everybody gets the same wage?

01:37:18.840 --> 01:37:23.281
I mean, there's so many conversations that
we could be having in this universe, instead

01:37:23.281 --> 01:37:25.340
of we're replacing all the truck drivers.

01:37:25.340 --> 01:37:26.390
They're going to vote for Republicans.

01:37:26.390 --> 01:37:34.090
I mean, we have such an impoverished imaginary
around how to have this conversation, it's

01:37:34.090 --> 01:37:39.760
as though we, in our-- I love the-- I'm coming
back to Adam's metaphor again of culture as

01:37:39.760 --> 01:37:40.970
this depositive memory.

01:37:40.970 --> 01:37:46.760
I mean, we've just forgotten the other part
of the collective conversation, which is the

01:37:46.760 --> 01:37:51.930
social collective that tries to make things
better for everybody in general.

01:37:51.930 --> 01:37:54.930
Not for a few people in particular.

01:37:54.930 --> 01:37:58.150
I mean, we've just kind of forgotten that
whole conversation.

01:37:58.150 --> 01:38:07.350
Well, for me, for example, the ways in which
like the Amazon Mechanical Turk, and all these,

01:38:07.350 --> 01:38:13.760
what I call just like this first factory,
I mean, in a way, it's like I think Marx's

01:38:13.760 --> 01:38:14.760
worst dream.

01:38:14.760 --> 01:38:21.940
Because it's 100% alienation, because everybody's
working in front of their computer screen.

01:38:21.940 --> 01:38:28.280
And the possibilities of unionization exists,
but are not as easy as people interacting

01:38:28.280 --> 01:38:29.280
physically.

01:38:29.280 --> 01:38:32.370
And of course, social media plays some role
in it.

01:38:32.370 --> 01:38:38.290
But I think one of the aspects of this topic
that interest me is flows of social energies,

01:38:38.290 --> 01:38:45.070
and how this changes with acceleration through
circulation or content and means and everything,

01:38:45.070 --> 01:38:51.970
social media, and how much we still need physical
bodies and physical interactions between people.

01:38:51.970 --> 01:38:57.239
And obviously, questions about this new working
class that is emerging in front of our eyes

01:38:57.239 --> 01:39:04.950
right now raises questions of, are we going
to have an equivalent of a revolution against

01:39:04.950 --> 01:39:11.989
this, and against the exploitations in this
new emerging working class?

01:39:11.989 --> 01:39:18.000
And whether the platforms such as social media
will allow for an accelerated process for

01:39:18.000 --> 01:39:24.620
people to unionize or do something else in
order to make the world better, make the working

01:39:24.620 --> 01:39:28.590
conditions better, and equitable, and so on
or not.

01:39:28.590 --> 01:39:35.520
And another point that I wanted to also answer
to, what Caroline has been saying about, the

01:39:35.520 --> 01:39:41.270
questions of how can the social make things
better is through collective intelligence.

01:39:41.270 --> 01:39:46.210
The reason why I'm, in my working, employing
this idea of collective Tamagotchi that I

01:39:46.210 --> 01:39:53.400
was thinking a lot about is that I realized
that there were about, I think, 76 million

01:39:53.400 --> 01:39:58.440
Tamagotchis-- so these toys in the shape--
the egg shaped toys-- sold worldwide.

01:39:58.440 --> 01:40:00.700
76 million.

01:40:00.700 --> 01:40:05.260
But what if, actually, one could imagine that
all these people that built these Tamagotchis

01:40:05.260 --> 01:40:07.820
would just take care of just one Tamagotchi?

01:40:07.820 --> 01:40:09.090
And we have this Tamagotchi.

01:40:09.090 --> 01:40:10.400
It's the plant earth, basically.

01:40:10.400 --> 01:40:14.870
It's also an egg shaped that we can take care
all.

01:40:14.870 --> 01:40:21.400
And obviously, people are self-organizing
in various ways to take care of this collective

01:40:21.400 --> 01:40:23.730
Tamagotchi.

01:40:23.730 --> 01:40:26.350
And this is a process of collective intelligence--
self-organization.

01:40:26.350 --> 01:40:27.840
It's an emergent process.

01:40:27.840 --> 01:40:31.480
Not everything is just top down organized
by politicians and governments.

01:40:31.480 --> 01:40:33.130
But there are things.

01:40:33.130 --> 01:40:38.130
And maybe, to answer your earlier question
about is there anything positive anyway, as

01:40:38.130 --> 01:40:43.820
we're coming probably towards an end [INAUDIBLE]
this conversation, there maybe is something

01:40:43.820 --> 01:40:44.820
positive.

01:40:44.820 --> 01:40:51.520
It's that so many people around the world
are involving and self-organizing into this

01:40:51.520 --> 01:40:55.570
debate about climate change, which I think
is positive.

01:40:55.570 --> 01:40:56.570
Mm-hmm.

01:40:56.570 --> 01:41:01.460
Well, and that's obviously one of the many
topics that Caroline mentioned that we have

01:41:01.460 --> 01:41:06.940
to discuss, as well as things more directly
rising from the conversation we've had.

01:41:06.940 --> 01:41:11.920
So I think we should move to informal conversation
at this point.

01:41:11.920 --> 01:41:13.140
I think we should conclude.

01:41:13.140 --> 01:41:16.190
Thanks to those who came, and thanks to the
the panelists.

01:41:16.190 --> 01:41:17.190
Thanks for listening.

01:41:17.190 --> 01:41:17.191
[APPLAUSE]

